1
00:00:29,246 --> 00:00:32,239
<i>It's an important and popular fact</i>

2
00:00:32,327 --> 00:00:36,038
<i>that things are not always
what they seem.</i>

3
00:00:36,129 --> 00:00:39,566
<i>For instance, on the planet Earth,
man had always assumed</i>

4
00:00:39,650 --> 00:00:43,326
<i>that he was the most intelligent
species occupying the planet,</i>

5
00:00:43,411 --> 00:00:45,972
<i>instead of the third most intelligent.</i>

6
00:00:46,053 --> 00:00:50,366
<i>The second most intelligent creatures
were dolphins, who, curiously enough,</i>

7
00:00:50,454 --> 00:00:54,131
<i>had long known of the impending
destruction of the planet Earth.</i>

8
00:00:54,216 --> 00:00:56,526
<i>They'd made many attempts
to alert mankind,</i>

9
00:00:56,617 --> 00:00:59,496
<i>but most of their communications
were misinterpreted</i>

10
00:00:59,578 --> 00:01:02,935
<i>as amusing attempts to punch footballs
or whistle for titbits.</i>

11
00:01:03,019 --> 00:01:07,333
<i>So they eventually decided they would
leave Earth by their own means.</i>

12
00:01:07,421 --> 00:01:10,459
<i>The last ever dolphin message
was misinterpreted</i>

13
00:01:10,542 --> 00:01:13,660
<i>as a sophisticated attempt
to do a backward somersault</i>

14
00:01:13,783 --> 00:01:16,662
<i>through a hoop,
whistling The Star-Spangled Banner.</i>

15
00:01:16,784 --> 00:01:18,423
<i>In fact, the message was this:</i>

16
00:01:18,545 --> 00:01:21,299
<i>"So long and thanks for all the fish."</i>

17
00:01:46,876 --> 00:01:48,833
<i>So long and thanks for all the fish</i>

18
00:01:48,917 --> 00:01:50,874
<i>So sad that it should come to this</i>

19
00:01:50,998 --> 00:01:54,912
<i>We tried to warn you all but, oh, dear</i>

20
00:01:55,039 --> 00:01:56,996
<i>You may not share our intellect</i>

21
00:01:57,080 --> 00:01:59,037
<i>Which might explain your disrespect</i>

22
00:01:59,121 --> 00:02:03,081
<i>For all the natural wonders
that grow around you</i>

23
00:02:03,162 --> 00:02:07,282
<i>So long, so long and thanks
for all the fish</i>

24
00:02:09,405 --> 00:02:11,203
<i>Your world's about to be destroyed</i>

25
00:02:11,286 --> 00:02:13,322
<i>There's no point getting all annoyed</i>

26
00:02:13,406 --> 00:02:17,720
<i>Lie back and let the planet
dissolve around you</i>

27
00:02:17,808 --> 00:02:19,606
<i>Despite those nets of tuna fleets</i>

28
00:02:19,689 --> 00:02:21,601
<i>We thought that most of you were sweet</i>

29
00:02:21,690 --> 00:02:25,604
<i>Especially tiny tots
and your pregnant women</i>

30
00:02:25,691 --> 00:02:27,808
<i>So long, so long,
so long, so long, so long</i>

31
00:02:29,733 --> 00:02:33,852
<i>So long, so long
and thanks for all the fish</i>

32
00:02:36,095 --> 00:02:40,170
<i>If I had just one last wish</i>

33
00:02:40,257 --> 00:02:44,137
<i>I would like a tasty fish</i>

34
00:02:44,218 --> 00:02:48,338
<i>If we could just change one thing</i>

35
00:02:48,420 --> 00:02:52,460
<i>We would all have lungs to sing</i>

36
00:02:52,582 --> 00:02:56,462
<i>Come one and all</i>

37
00:02:56,583 --> 00:03:00,658
<i>Man and mammal</i>

38
00:03:00,745 --> 00:03:03,818
<i>Side by side</i>

39
00:03:03,906 --> 00:03:09,858
<i>In life's great gene pool</i>

40
00:03:27,995 --> 00:03:30,066
<i>So long, so long,
so long, so long, so long</i>

41
00:03:32,037 --> 00:03:37,989
<i>So long, so long
and thanks for all the fish</i>

42
00:03:50,924 --> 00:03:55,124
<i>The extraordinary story
of The Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy</i>

43
00:03:55,206 --> 00:03:56,798
<i>begins very simply.</i>

44
00:03:56,886 --> 00:03:59,641
<i>It begins with a man.</i>

45
00:04:08,331 --> 00:04:11,847
<i>An Earthman, to be precise,
who no more knows his destiny,</i>

46
00:04:11,932 --> 00:04:15,244
<i>than a tea leaf knows the history
of the East India Company.</i>

47
00:04:17,494 --> 00:04:20,168
<i>His name is Arthur Dent.</i>

48
00:04:20,256 --> 00:04:23,408
<i>He is a five-foot-eight-inch-tall
ape descendant,</i>

49
00:04:23,497 --> 00:04:27,332
<i>and someone is trying to drive
a bypass through his house.</i>

50
00:04:32,420 --> 00:04:34,332
What do I want you to do about it?

51
00:04:34,421 --> 00:04:38,416
Find him and tell him I'm currently
lying flat on my back in front of...

52
00:04:38,503 --> 00:04:39,731
Fine, I'll hold.

53
00:04:39,823 --> 00:04:42,384
You can't lie in front
of the bulldozers for ever.

54
00:04:42,464 --> 00:04:44,535
I'm game. We'll see who rusts first.

55
00:04:44,625 --> 00:04:47,823
This bypass has got to be built
and it is going to be built.

56
00:04:47,906 --> 00:04:51,742
- Why has it got to be built?
- It's a bypass.

57
00:04:51,868 --> 00:04:56,226
You've got to build bypasses. Besides,
you should've protested months ago.

58
00:04:56,309 --> 00:04:59,508
The plans have been on display
at the planning office for a year.

59
00:04:59,591 --> 00:05:02,152
On display?
I had to go down to a cellar.

60
00:05:02,272 --> 00:05:07,394
Mr Dent, have you any idea how much
damage this bulldozer would suffer

61
00:05:07,474 --> 00:05:09,830
if I just let it roll straight over you?

62
00:05:10,995 --> 00:05:13,829
- How much?
- None at all.

63
00:05:13,916 --> 00:05:16,636
<i>By a strange coincidence,
"none at all"</i>

64
00:05:16,717 --> 00:05:20,268
<i>is exactly how much suspicion
ape-descendant Arthur Dent had</i>

65
00:05:20,359 --> 00:05:22,271
<i>that one of his closest friends</i>

66
00:05:22,360 --> 00:05:25,876
<i>was not descended from an ape,
but was, in fact, from a small planet</i>

67
00:05:25,961 --> 00:05:28,954
<i>somewhere in the vicinity
of Betelgeuse.</i>

68
00:05:29,722 --> 00:05:32,112
Arthur.

69
00:05:32,203 --> 00:05:33,876
- Ford.
- Yes?

70
00:05:33,964 --> 00:05:35,398
Here.

71
00:05:35,485 --> 00:05:36,759
There you are.

72
00:05:36,845 --> 00:05:38,598
Eat, drink. We gotta talk.

73
00:05:38,686 --> 00:05:41,804
Now's not the best time.
They're going to demolish my home.

74
00:05:43,288 --> 00:05:46,122
You already know? How?

75
00:05:46,209 --> 00:05:48,041
What do you...?

76
00:05:49,730 --> 00:05:52,689
Oh, they, they? When you say
"they" you mean they? Got it.

77
00:05:52,771 --> 00:05:57,244
Listen. I got somethin' important
I gotta tell you. Right now.

78
00:05:57,333 --> 00:05:59,723
Well, what about my house?

79
00:06:04,056 --> 00:06:09,337
Workers of the Earth, I bring
good tidings of peanuts and beer.

80
00:06:19,462 --> 00:06:21,214
Good. Let's go to the pub.

81
00:06:21,302 --> 00:06:24,182
They won't demolish it
until they've finished the beers.

82
00:06:24,264 --> 00:06:26,539
- Can we trust 'em?
- To the end of the Earth.

83
00:06:26,664 --> 00:06:28,940
- How far's that?
- About 12 minutes away.

84
00:06:30,106 --> 00:06:33,895
Barman, six pints of bitter and quickly.
The world's about to end.

85
00:06:33,987 --> 00:06:35,819
Six pints coming up.

86
00:06:35,908 --> 00:06:39,424
Keep the change.
You got about ten minutes to spend it.

87
00:06:39,549 --> 00:06:42,748
Three pints each? At lunchtime?

88
00:06:49,553 --> 00:06:51,510
Sorry.

89
00:06:51,594 --> 00:06:53,825
Time is an illusion.

90
00:06:53,915 --> 00:06:55,986
Lunchtime, doubly so.

91
00:06:56,076 --> 00:06:59,752
And eat those peanuts
because you'll need the salt.

92
00:06:59,837 --> 00:07:02,398
- Look, what is going on, Ford?
- Arthur...

93
00:07:04,439 --> 00:07:07,750
What if I told you
I really wasn't from Guildford?

94
00:07:07,840 --> 00:07:12,074
I was from a small planet
somewhere in the vicinity of Betelgeuse?

95
00:07:12,202 --> 00:07:15,559
- Is it something you're likely to say?
- Remember when we met?

96
00:07:22,286 --> 00:07:23,435
Out of the way.

97
00:07:26,888 --> 00:07:27,877
Hi.

98
00:07:27,968 --> 00:07:30,881
Wasn't it strange I was trying
to shake hands with a car?

99
00:07:30,969 --> 00:07:32,449
I assumed you were drunk.

100
00:07:32,530 --> 00:07:36,445
I thought cars were the dominant
life form. I was introducing myself.

101
00:07:36,572 --> 00:07:40,771
You saved my life.
And now I'm saving yours. Please drink.

102
00:07:40,853 --> 00:07:44,051
It must be Thursday. I could never
get the hang of Thursdays.

103
00:07:44,135 --> 00:07:47,367
- If this is about your house...
- No, it's not about the house.

104
00:07:51,217 --> 00:07:53,254
- Who's he?
- She.

105
00:07:53,338 --> 00:07:56,809
Tricia McMillan.
We met at a fancy-dress party.

106
00:07:58,180 --> 00:08:00,092
<i>I hate those kind of parties.</i>

107
00:08:00,181 --> 00:08:03,060
<i>I'd rather have stayed at home
and ironed my hankies.</i>

108
00:08:03,142 --> 00:08:05,213
<i>- But there I was.
- Who are you?</i>

109
00:08:05,303 --> 00:08:06,975
<i>And there she was.</i>

110
00:08:07,063 --> 00:08:11,581
- Dent. Arthur Dent.
- No. I mean, who are you?

111
00:08:11,665 --> 00:08:14,783
Oh, the costume? Right.
"Livingstone, I presume."

112
00:08:15,907 --> 00:08:20,060
Yeah, it's not as clever as Darwin,
but the best I could do at short notice.

113
00:08:20,148 --> 00:08:22,105
You're the first to get that right.

114
00:08:22,189 --> 00:08:24,863
- Really?
- Yes. Everyone calls me Santa.

115
00:08:24,950 --> 00:08:27,340
- Right.
- I thought the beagle was a giveaway.

116
00:08:27,471 --> 00:08:29,030
So did I.

117
00:08:29,112 --> 00:08:31,422
People at these parties
are drunken idiots.

118
00:08:31,513 --> 00:08:32,832
What?

119
00:08:32,913 --> 00:08:35,474
All these people are idiots. God.

120
00:08:39,116 --> 00:08:40,754
That's awkward.

121
00:08:40,836 --> 00:08:42,555
Tell me.

122
00:08:44,398 --> 00:08:47,038
She was amazing though, Ford.

123
00:08:47,159 --> 00:08:49,993
<i>Beautiful, witty, mad as a balloon.</i>

124
00:08:50,080 --> 00:08:54,519
Without the beard,
you look at least 80 years younger.

125
00:08:54,642 --> 00:08:57,282
Maybe I'm de-evolving.

126
00:08:59,964 --> 00:09:05,404
Well, I should tell you that I do not
date single-celled organisms, OK?

127
00:09:08,007 --> 00:09:10,124
Let's go somewhere.

128
00:09:10,208 --> 00:09:14,168
Yeah. Definitely.
Where do you have in mind?

129
00:09:14,249 --> 00:09:18,449
- Madagascar.
- Is that that new club on Dean Street?

130
00:09:18,531 --> 00:09:21,330
No, it's a country
off the coast of Africa.

131
00:09:21,412 --> 00:09:24,291
That Madagascar?
Why are we waiting here?

132
00:09:24,854 --> 00:09:27,243
Wait a sec.

133
00:09:27,334 --> 00:09:28,848
Go.

134
00:09:31,856 --> 00:09:36,170
God, you're serious.
I can't go to Madagascar.

135
00:09:36,258 --> 00:09:39,171
- Why not?
- Because, I just, you know...

136
00:09:39,259 --> 00:09:40,579
- You're serious?
- Yeah.

137
00:09:40,660 --> 00:09:44,131
I want to go somewhere I've never been
and I'd like to go with you,

138
00:09:44,221 --> 00:09:46,861
so what do you say, Dr Livingstone?

139
00:09:47,942 --> 00:09:52,540
I say that's
an extraordinary proposition.

140
00:09:52,624 --> 00:09:55,503
I can't go. I mean, I've got a job.

141
00:09:55,585 --> 00:09:57,736
Quit. Get a new one
when you come back.

142
00:09:57,866 --> 00:09:59,778
I don't even know your real name.

143
00:09:59,867 --> 00:10:03,065
- Tricia McMillan.
- Well, Tricia McMillan.

144
00:10:03,989 --> 00:10:06,424
I have a proposition for you.

145
00:10:06,550 --> 00:10:13,151
Why don't we go somewhere
a little closer first, say Cornwall,

146
00:10:13,232 --> 00:10:16,943
and we'll see how it goes.

147
00:10:17,034 --> 00:10:21,426
Right, of course, Cornwall.

148
00:10:21,515 --> 00:10:23,632
Hey, excuse me.

149
00:10:26,397 --> 00:10:30,392
Is this guy boring you?
Why don't you talk to me instead?

150
00:10:30,479 --> 00:10:33,233
I'm from a different planet.

151
00:10:36,201 --> 00:10:39,239
It's true. You want to see my spaceship?

152
00:10:40,683 --> 00:10:45,236
"Do you want to see my spaceship?"
What kind of chat-up line is that?

153
00:10:45,325 --> 00:10:47,316
That does happen.

154
00:10:47,406 --> 00:10:49,317
Speaking of...

155
00:10:50,367 --> 00:10:52,518
We've got two minutes.
Drink up.

156
00:10:53,208 --> 00:10:55,484
That's my house.

157
00:11:01,971 --> 00:11:05,852
People of Earth, a round of drinks,
for everyone, on me.

158
00:11:05,933 --> 00:11:08,494
You really think
the world's going to end?

159
00:11:08,574 --> 00:11:10,053
Yes.

160
00:11:10,615 --> 00:11:14,404
Shouldn't we lie down, or put a paper
bag over our heads, or something?

161
00:11:14,496 --> 00:11:17,295
- If you like.
- Will it help?

162
00:11:17,377 --> 00:11:19,972
Not at all. See you.

163
00:11:20,058 --> 00:11:21,253
Last orders, then.

164
00:11:36,905 --> 00:11:39,136
- What is that?
- Run.

165
00:11:39,226 --> 00:11:41,103
Look out.

166
00:12:08,317 --> 00:12:09,875
Towel.

167
00:12:10,758 --> 00:12:12,670
Arthur.

168
00:12:12,759 --> 00:12:14,636
What the hell are those things?

169
00:12:15,520 --> 00:12:18,115
They're ships
from a Vogon constructor fleet.

170
00:12:18,201 --> 00:12:22,593
I picked up their signal this morning.
Here, you'll need this.

171
00:12:22,682 --> 00:12:25,721
- What are you doing?
- We're hitching a ride.

172
00:12:28,805 --> 00:12:33,004
People of Earth,
this is Prostetnic Vogon Jeltz

173
00:12:33,086 --> 00:12:35,647
of the Galactic Hyperspace
Planning Council.

174
00:12:35,727 --> 00:12:37,207
As you are probably aware,

175
00:12:37,288 --> 00:12:40,759
plans for the development
of the outlying regions of the galaxy

176
00:12:40,849 --> 00:12:45,208
involve the building of a hyperspace
express route through your star system.

177
00:12:45,291 --> 00:12:49,286
And your planet is one of those
scheduled for demolition.

178
00:12:57,016 --> 00:12:59,406
<i>There's no point
acting surprised about it.</i>

179
00:12:59,497 --> 00:13:01,932
<i>The plans have been on display</i>

180
00:13:02,018 --> 00:13:05,614
<i>at your local planning office
in Alpha Centauri for 50 Earth years.</i>

181
00:13:06,459 --> 00:13:10,249
<i>If you can't be bothered
with local affairs, that's your lookout.</i>

182
00:13:10,341 --> 00:13:12,697
Apathetic bloody planet.

183
00:13:12,782 --> 00:13:15,661
I've no sympathy at all.

184
00:14:58,743 --> 00:15:01,338
The Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy

185
00:15:01,424 --> 00:15:03,780
<i>is a wholly remarkable book.</i>

186
00:15:03,865 --> 00:15:06,983
<i>Perhaps the most remarkable,
certainly the most successful,</i>

187
00:15:07,066 --> 00:15:10,981
<i>ever to come out of the great
publishing corporations of Ursa Minor.</i>

188
00:15:11,068 --> 00:15:14,220
<i>More popular than
The Celestial Homecare Omnibus,</i>

189
00:15:14,309 --> 00:15:17,939
<i>better selling than
53 More Things to Do in Zero Gravity,</i>

190
00:15:18,030 --> 00:15:20,909
<i>and more controversial than
Oolon Colluphid's trilogy</i>

191
00:15:20,992 --> 00:15:24,030
<i>of philosophical blockbusters,
Where God Went Wrong,</i>

192
00:15:24,113 --> 00:15:26,503
Some More of God's Greatest Mistakes,

193
00:15:26,594 --> 00:15:28,825
<i>and Who Is This God Person Anyway?</i>

194
00:15:29,475 --> 00:15:32,468
<i>It's already supplanted
the Encyclopaedia Galactica</i>

195
00:15:32,596 --> 00:15:35,589
<i>as the standard repository
of all knowledge and wisdom</i>

196
00:15:35,677 --> 00:15:37,953
<i>for two important reasons.</i>

197
00:15:38,038 --> 00:15:40,507
<i>First, it's slightly cheaper,</i>

198
00:15:40,639 --> 00:15:43,598
<i>and second, it has the words
"Don't Panic"</i>

199
00:15:43,680 --> 00:15:46,992
<i>printed in large,
friendly letters on its cover.</i>

200
00:15:55,325 --> 00:15:57,999
So you're not from Guildford?

201
00:15:58,126 --> 00:16:02,006
Which would explain the accent,
which I've always wondered about.

202
00:16:02,087 --> 00:16:05,684
You're not an out-of-work actor,

203
00:16:05,769 --> 00:16:11,050
but rather a writer for this,
this book thing.

204
00:16:11,811 --> 00:16:13,450
Good, huh?

205
00:16:13,532 --> 00:16:15,842
I don't feel well.
I need a cup of tea.

206
00:16:15,933 --> 00:16:19,210
If I asked where we were,
would I regret it?

207
00:16:19,294 --> 00:16:22,287
- We're safe for now.
- Good.

208
00:16:22,375 --> 00:16:26,165
We're in the washroom on one of the
ships of the Vogon constructor fleet.

209
00:16:26,297 --> 00:16:29,608
- Get me home, Ford.
- Arthur, your home is...

210
00:16:29,738 --> 00:16:34,097
Oh, God, my home.
My home was demolished.

211
00:16:38,782 --> 00:16:40,693
You don't remember.

212
00:16:41,623 --> 00:16:47,700
OK, Arthur, I've got something to tell
you. It's unfortunate, but it's true.

213
00:16:47,785 --> 00:16:50,823
Your home planet has been blown up.

214
00:16:59,310 --> 00:17:01,221
Blown up.

215
00:17:07,793 --> 00:17:09,785
Couldn't you have done something?

216
00:17:09,874 --> 00:17:13,754
I saved your life.
OK, that makes us even.

217
00:17:13,835 --> 00:17:16,987
It's a tough galaxy. If you want
to survive out here,

218
00:17:17,077 --> 00:17:21,151
you've gotta know where your towel is.

219
00:17:21,278 --> 00:17:23,668
OK, give me a hand over here.

220
00:17:26,040 --> 00:17:29,920
Careful. It's hot.
We've gotta get off this ship.

221
00:17:30,002 --> 00:17:31,958
Before the Vogons find us.

222
00:17:32,042 --> 00:17:34,955
Vogons, they hate hitchhikers. Pull.

223
00:17:40,526 --> 00:17:43,724
- Now we'll get a signal.
- What is a Vogon?

224
00:17:43,807 --> 00:17:45,605
Ask the Guide. Say "Vogons".

225
00:17:47,288 --> 00:17:49,280
Vogons.

226
00:17:50,049 --> 00:17:54,249
<i>Vogons are one of the
most unpleasant races in the galaxy.</i>

227
00:17:54,331 --> 00:17:58,451
<i>Not evil, but bad-tempered,
bureaucratic, officious and callous.</i>

228
00:17:58,533 --> 00:18:01,970
<i>They wouldn't even lift a finger
to save their own grandmothers</i>

229
00:18:02,054 --> 00:18:05,127
<i>from the Ravenous Bugblatter Beast
of Traal without orders</i>

230
00:18:05,215 --> 00:18:07,571
<i>signed in triplicate,
sent in, sent back,</i>

231
00:18:07,656 --> 00:18:10,854
<i>queried, lost, found,
subjected to public inquiry,</i>

232
00:18:10,977 --> 00:18:14,210
<i>lost again and finally buried
in soft peat for three months</i>

233
00:18:14,299 --> 00:18:17,053
<i>and recycled as firelighters.</i>

234
00:18:18,300 --> 00:18:22,898
<i>On no account should you allow
a Vogon to read poetry to you.</i>

235
00:18:24,503 --> 00:18:28,895
They can't think or imagine.
Most can't spell. They just run things.

236
00:18:34,947 --> 00:18:36,858
And if we don't get a ride soon,

237
00:18:36,947 --> 00:18:40,225
we won't need the Guide to tell us
how unpleasant the Vogons are.

238
00:18:40,309 --> 00:18:42,540
They've already destroyed
a planet today.

239
00:18:42,630 --> 00:18:44,861
That always makes them a little...

240
00:18:50,993 --> 00:18:53,906
- What is that?
- Put this in your ear.

241
00:18:58,276 --> 00:19:01,952
<i>We have unwittingly picked up
a couple of hitchhikers.</i>

242
00:19:02,037 --> 00:19:04,632
The fish is translating for you.

243
00:19:05,559 --> 00:19:08,552
<i>The Babel fish
is small, yellow, leech-like,</i>

244
00:19:08,640 --> 00:19:12,793
<i>and probably the oddest thing
in the universe.</i>

245
00:19:12,881 --> 00:19:17,161
<i>It feeds on brainwave energy,
absorbing unconscious frequencies,</i>

246
00:19:17,243 --> 00:19:19,712
<i>and excreting a matrix
of conscious frequencies</i>

247
00:19:19,844 --> 00:19:21,915
<i>to the speech centres of the brain.</i>

248
00:19:22,045 --> 00:19:25,402
<i>The practical upshot of which is that,
if you stick one in your ear,</i>

249
00:19:25,486 --> 00:19:30,881
<i>you instantly understand
anything said to you in any language.</i>

250
00:19:38,651 --> 00:19:41,041
Resistance is useless.

251
00:19:44,694 --> 00:19:46,685
- All right.
- Someone's coming.

252
00:19:46,815 --> 00:19:49,090
Here we go. Look lively.

253
00:20:25,029 --> 00:20:26,509
Sir.

254
00:20:26,590 --> 00:20:29,230
- What?
- Hitchhikers.

255
00:21:07,046 --> 00:21:10,164
"O freddled gruntbuggly..."

256
00:21:10,247 --> 00:21:12,682
No, really, you don't have to read.

257
00:21:12,768 --> 00:21:15,363
We've put you through
enough trouble already.

258
00:21:15,449 --> 00:21:20,400
"...as plerdled gabbleblotchits
on a lurgid bee."

259
00:21:20,491 --> 00:21:25,613
<i>Vogon poetry is widely accepted
as the third worst in the universe.</i>

260
00:21:26,733 --> 00:21:29,851
<i>The second worst
is that of the Azgoths of Kria.</i>

261
00:21:29,934 --> 00:21:33,326
<i>During a recitation by their
Poet Master, Grunthos the Flatulent,</i>

262
00:21:33,416 --> 00:21:36,215
<i>of his poem
Ode to a Small Lump of Green Putty</i>

263
00:21:36,297 --> 00:21:38,766
I Found in my Armpit
One Midsummer Morning,

264
00:21:38,898 --> 00:21:41,971
<i>four of his audience
died of internal haemorrhaging,</i>

265
00:21:42,059 --> 00:21:45,416
<i>and the President of the
Mid-Galactic Arts Nobbling Council</i>

266
00:21:45,501 --> 00:21:47,810
<i>survived by gnawing
one of his own legs off.</i>

267
00:21:47,901 --> 00:21:52,613
"Or I will rend thee
in the gobberwarts..."

268
00:21:52,703 --> 00:21:55,537
"...with my blurgle..."

269
00:21:55,624 --> 00:21:59,858
<i>The absolute worst poetry was by
Paula Millstone Jennings of Sussex.</i>

270
00:21:59,946 --> 00:22:02,336
<i>Luckily, it was destroyed
when the Earth was.</i>

271
00:22:02,467 --> 00:22:06,940
"See if I don't." Yeah.

272
00:22:09,030 --> 00:22:14,186
So, Earthlings,
I present you with a choice.

273
00:22:14,272 --> 00:22:17,265
Either die in the vacuum of space,

274
00:22:17,393 --> 00:22:21,991
or tell me what you thought of my poem.

275
00:22:23,555 --> 00:22:27,311
Actually, I rather liked it.

276
00:22:29,398 --> 00:22:31,993
That's good. Run with it.

277
00:22:32,079 --> 00:22:34,388
Some of the words I didn't understand,

278
00:22:34,520 --> 00:22:37,752
but I found the imagery quite effective.

279
00:22:38,641 --> 00:22:39,916
Continue.

280
00:22:40,002 --> 00:22:43,518
Well, yes,
interesting rhythmic devices,

281
00:22:43,603 --> 00:22:45,959
which seemed to counterpoint

282
00:22:46,044 --> 00:22:48,605
the underlying metaphor
of the humanity of...

283
00:22:48,685 --> 00:22:51,758
- Vogonity.
- Vogonity, sorry, vogonity

284
00:22:51,846 --> 00:22:54,236
of the poet's soul.

285
00:22:57,648 --> 00:23:01,609
So what you're saying is,

286
00:23:01,690 --> 00:23:04,159
I write poetry because

287
00:23:04,291 --> 00:23:08,525
underneath this mean,
callous, heartless exterior,

288
00:23:08,613 --> 00:23:12,402
I just want to be loved?

289
00:23:13,615 --> 00:23:15,571
Yes.

290
00:23:15,655 --> 00:23:18,011
Yes, yeah, please.

291
00:23:23,378 --> 00:23:24,892
Throw them off the ship.

292
00:23:24,979 --> 00:23:28,256
- No.
- Ford.

293
00:23:29,821 --> 00:23:32,017
All right.

294
00:23:32,102 --> 00:23:35,493
Resistance is useless.

295
00:23:38,064 --> 00:23:42,378
Shut up. Get a job.
Wash your filthy hands.

296
00:23:42,506 --> 00:23:45,101
Don't panic.

297
00:23:45,187 --> 00:23:50,946
So this is it?
We're gonna die?

298
00:23:51,029 --> 00:23:54,500
Yeah, we're gonna die.

299
00:23:54,591 --> 00:23:57,390
No, what's this?

300
00:23:57,472 --> 00:23:59,064
What is this?

301
00:23:59,152 --> 00:24:03,272
What's this? This is nothing.
Yeah, we're gonna die.

302
00:24:13,518 --> 00:24:15,555
You're sweating.

303
00:24:23,482 --> 00:24:25,633
- Would you like a hug?
- No.

304
00:24:38,648 --> 00:24:42,085
<i>"Space," says the introduction
to The Hitchhiker's Guide,</i>

305
00:24:42,169 --> 00:24:44,525
<i>"is big. Really big."</i>

306
00:24:44,610 --> 00:24:49,811
<i>"You just won't believe how vastly,
hugely, mind-bogglingly big it is. "</i>

307
00:24:49,892 --> 00:24:51,690
<i>And so on.</i>

308
00:24:51,773 --> 00:24:54,686
<i>It also says that
if you hold a lungful of air,</i>

309
00:24:54,774 --> 00:24:59,975
<i>you can survive in the total vacuum
of space for about 30 seconds.</i>

310
00:25:00,056 --> 00:25:02,127
<i>But with space being really big and all,</i>

311
00:25:02,257 --> 00:25:04,692
<i>the chances of being picked up
within that time</i>

312
00:25:04,778 --> 00:25:06,849
<i>are two to the power of two billion,</i>

313
00:25:06,939 --> 00:25:09,852
<i>79 million, 460 thousand,</i>

314
00:25:09,980 --> 00:25:12,814
<i>347 to one against.</i>

315
00:25:12,901 --> 00:25:14,620
<i>By a staggering coincidence,</i>

316
00:25:14,742 --> 00:25:17,211
<i>it's also the phone number
of the Islington flat</i>

317
00:25:17,343 --> 00:25:19,459
<i>where Arthur went to a fancy dress party</i>

318
00:25:19,543 --> 00:25:22,742
<i>and met a very nice young woman
whom he totally blew it with.</i>

319
00:25:26,106 --> 00:25:29,986
<i>Though the planet Earth,
the Islington flat and telephone</i>

320
00:25:30,068 --> 00:25:35,463
<i>have all now been demolished,
Ford and Arthur were, in fact, rescued.</i>

321
00:25:35,550 --> 00:25:38,270
- Ford?
- Yes.

322
00:25:38,351 --> 00:25:42,265
- I think I'm a sofa.
- I know how you feel.

323
00:25:49,395 --> 00:25:51,990
So much for the laws of physics.

324
00:25:52,076 --> 00:25:53,829
<i>And once again, our top story.</i>

325
00:25:53,957 --> 00:25:58,076
<i>The sensational theft of the most
coveted ship in the universe,</i>

326
00:25:58,158 --> 00:25:59,990
<i>the starship Heart of Gold.</i>

327
00:26:00,079 --> 00:26:02,514
<i>Stolen at the launch ceremony
by none other</i>

328
00:26:02,600 --> 00:26:05,320
<i>than Galactic President
Zaphod Beeblebrox.</i>

329
00:26:05,401 --> 00:26:10,477
<i>In the name of people, freedom,
and democracy, stuff like that,</i>

330
00:26:10,563 --> 00:26:14,444
<i>I hereby kidnap myself
and I'm taking the ship with me.</i>

331
00:26:14,525 --> 00:26:16,482
<i>Come on.</i>

332
00:26:16,566 --> 00:26:18,921
<i>Beeblebrox, universally considered</i>

333
00:26:19,006 --> 00:26:21,841
<i>to be the dimmest star
in several solar systems,</i>

334
00:26:21,928 --> 00:26:25,444
<i>is most famous for his controversial
defeat of Humma Kavula</i>

335
00:26:25,529 --> 00:26:29,318
<i>who claimed many thought they were
voting for the worst-dressed being</i>

336
00:26:29,411 --> 00:26:31,402
<i>in the universe contest.</i>

337
00:26:32,412 --> 00:26:33,401
Great.

338
00:26:33,492 --> 00:26:36,291
<i>Kavula is best remembered
for his slanderous</i>

339
00:26:36,413 --> 00:26:38,848
<i>"Don't Vote for Stupid!" campaign.</i>

340
00:26:38,934 --> 00:26:42,292
Put your ego aside.
Something important has happened.

341
00:26:42,376 --> 00:26:46,575
If there's anything more important than
my ego, I want it caught and shot now.

342
00:26:46,697 --> 00:26:48,290
Come on, I love it.

343
00:26:48,378 --> 00:26:51,291
<i>Zaphod's just this guy, you know.</i>

344
00:26:54,060 --> 00:26:55,049
What d'you...?

345
00:26:55,181 --> 00:26:59,255
- I was just watching myself.
- We have a couple of hitchhikers.

346
00:26:59,342 --> 00:27:01,811
Hitchhikers?
Why'd you pick up hitchhikers?

347
00:27:01,903 --> 00:27:03,701
I didn't. The ship did.

348
00:27:03,824 --> 00:27:05,941
What? Say what?

349
00:27:06,025 --> 00:27:10,463
When we engaged the
lmprobability Drive... The big button.

350
00:27:10,546 --> 00:27:12,503
- I know.
- They were picked up here.

351
00:27:12,587 --> 00:27:16,787
In sector ZZ9 Plural Z Alpha. Wait.
That's where you picked me up.

352
00:27:16,869 --> 00:27:18,701
- On Earth.
- That's impossible.

353
00:27:18,790 --> 00:27:21,988
- No, just very improbable.
- Listen. I don't have time for this.

354
00:27:22,111 --> 00:27:26,186
We've got the police of half the galaxy
after us. We've stopped for hitchhikers.

355
00:27:26,273 --> 00:27:28,503
So ten out of ten for style, but

356
00:27:28,593 --> 00:27:30,949
minus several million
for good thinking.

357
00:27:31,034 --> 00:27:34,187
You're too gorgeous, baby, stop it.
You drive me crazy.

358
00:27:35,196 --> 00:27:37,506
Don't. I'll send Marvin.

359
00:27:38,677 --> 00:27:40,396
Marvin.

360
00:27:43,399 --> 00:27:46,198
You ought to know,
I'm feeling very depressed.

361
00:27:47,281 --> 00:27:50,479
Well, we have something
that should take your mind off things.

362
00:27:50,562 --> 00:27:55,035
It won't work.
I have an exceptionally large mind.

363
00:27:55,164 --> 00:28:00,843
Yeah, we know. But we need you to go
down to the number two entry bay

364
00:28:00,966 --> 00:28:03,686
and pick up our stowaways
and bring them up here.

365
00:28:03,767 --> 00:28:06,919
Just that? I won't enjoy it.

366
00:28:07,008 --> 00:28:09,569
Yeah, well, that's life.

367
00:28:09,649 --> 00:28:13,724
Life? Don't talk to me about life.

368
00:28:22,534 --> 00:28:24,890
<i>- Commander Kwaltz.
- Yes.</i>

369
00:28:24,975 --> 00:28:29,129
<i>We have located the spaceship
Heart of Gold and President Beeblebrox.</i>

370
00:28:29,217 --> 00:28:33,177
<i>- Where?
- Sector ZZ9 Plural Z Alpha.</i>

371
00:28:33,259 --> 00:28:35,693
<i>Requesting hyperspace clearance.</i>

372
00:28:35,820 --> 00:28:41,454
Hold your position, captain,
until clearance is granted.

373
00:28:41,542 --> 00:28:45,695
<i>- Yes, sir.
- Get me Vice-President Questular.</i>

374
00:28:48,945 --> 00:28:52,985
<i>Hyperspace permission granted, captain.</i>

375
00:28:53,106 --> 00:28:55,337
Here we go.

376
00:29:16,075 --> 00:29:18,670
I think that door just sighed.

377
00:29:18,756 --> 00:29:21,066
Ghastly, isn't it?

378
00:29:21,157 --> 00:29:23,467
All the doors have been programmed

379
00:29:23,558 --> 00:29:26,631
to have a cheerful
and sunny disposition.

380
00:29:28,720 --> 00:29:33,318
Anyway, come on. I've been ordered
to take you up to the bridge.

381
00:29:35,082 --> 00:29:36,994
Please yourselves.

382
00:29:37,083 --> 00:29:40,201
Here I am,
brain the size of a planet,

383
00:29:40,284 --> 00:29:42,719
and they ask me to take you
up to the bridge.

384
00:29:42,845 --> 00:29:46,157
Call that job satisfaction?
Cos I don't.

385
00:29:46,247 --> 00:29:51,721
You can thank the Sirius Cybernetics
Corp. For building robots with GPP.

386
00:29:51,809 --> 00:29:55,166
- What's G.P. P?
- Genuine People Personalities.

387
00:29:55,330 --> 00:29:59,530
I'm a personality prototype.
You can tell, can't you?

388
00:30:05,414 --> 00:30:06,894
Arthur?

389
00:30:07,775 --> 00:30:12,168
<i>Hey, Slim, are you wearing
my underwear? Cos I'm wearing yours.</i>

390
00:30:12,257 --> 00:30:14,931
<i>And they ain't doing the trick.
Come on. All right.</i>

391
00:30:17,979 --> 00:30:19,971
Oh, for heaven's sake.

392
00:30:20,900 --> 00:30:23,699
I've brought the aliens.
Don't thank me or anything.

393
00:30:25,862 --> 00:30:27,819
Freeze.

394
00:30:27,943 --> 00:30:31,300
Freeze? I'm a robot. Not a refrigerator.

395
00:30:31,384 --> 00:30:34,856
- I wasn't talking to you, Giggles.
- Why do I bother?

396
00:30:34,946 --> 00:30:36,664
- Zaphod?
- Ford.

397
00:30:36,786 --> 00:30:40,986
Is that you? Ford. Praxibetel lx.

398
00:30:41,068 --> 00:30:42,980
What the hell are you doing here?

399
00:30:43,069 --> 00:30:46,346
I just stuck out my thumb and here I am.

400
00:30:46,470 --> 00:30:49,144
- That is so you.
- Look at you.

401
00:30:49,271 --> 00:30:53,471
President of the Galaxy.
I can't believe you beat Humma Kavula.

402
00:30:53,553 --> 00:30:55,111
You zarkin' frood.

403
00:30:55,194 --> 00:30:59,393
I want you to meet a friend.
Arthur, this is Zaphod Beeblebrox.

404
00:31:00,556 --> 00:31:05,347
President of the Galaxy. This is my
cousin. He's a semi-half brother...

405
00:31:05,437 --> 00:31:08,875
- We share three of the same mothers.
- We've met.

406
00:31:08,999 --> 00:31:14,234
Have we? I'm sorry.
I've got a terrible memory for species.

407
00:31:14,321 --> 00:31:18,475
This is him, Ford. The would-you-like-
to-see-my-spaceship bloke.

408
00:31:19,963 --> 00:31:24,993
- Hello, Arthur.
- Tricia.

409
00:31:25,085 --> 00:31:27,395
- How you doin'?
- Hey, Trillian.

410
00:31:27,486 --> 00:31:31,116
This is my semi-half-brother lx,
I'm sorry, Ford.

411
00:31:31,207 --> 00:31:33,881
- Hi.
- Hi.

412
00:31:34,009 --> 00:31:37,764
Would you excuse us
for a second, please?

413
00:31:37,850 --> 00:31:40,968
You went down on that little planet
and didn't call me?

414
00:31:41,051 --> 00:31:43,771
Sorry, I didn't know.
I had a galaxy to run.

415
00:31:43,852 --> 00:31:46,048
Hi, Tricia McMillan, right?

416
00:31:46,133 --> 00:31:49,171
I think he called you Trillian.
Which of us got the right one?

417
00:31:49,254 --> 00:31:51,723
I shortened it.
Something a little more spacey.

418
00:31:51,855 --> 00:31:54,290
Right. I was thinking
of changing mine to,

419
00:31:54,376 --> 00:31:56,254
I don't know, Arthoolia.

420
00:31:56,337 --> 00:32:00,218
That's a good one.
Well, this is weird.

421
00:32:00,299 --> 00:32:03,690
- How'd you get here?
- I just stuck out my thumb.

422
00:32:03,780 --> 00:32:07,251
- Right. In your pyjamas?
- I was in a hurry.

423
00:32:07,381 --> 00:32:09,373
OK. Did you know I was here?

424
00:32:09,462 --> 00:32:12,023
Don't flatter yourself.
I've got a spaceman too.

425
00:32:12,103 --> 00:32:14,698
Yeah, that's kinda like
the Dingo Shuffle, yeah.

426
00:32:14,824 --> 00:32:16,781
OK, look.

427
00:32:16,865 --> 00:32:20,542
I left you at the party. I feel bad, but
I was gonna call you when I got back.

428
00:32:20,627 --> 00:32:23,506
Well, there is no
going back now, is there?

429
00:32:24,588 --> 00:32:26,102
You do know what happened?

430
00:32:26,189 --> 00:32:28,863
Enough small talk.
We're on the run, remember?

431
00:32:28,950 --> 00:32:30,782
Excuse me, we're having a chat.

432
00:32:30,951 --> 00:32:33,226
Well, I think the girl's getting...
Boo.

433
00:32:33,352 --> 00:32:38,428
You blew it with her, Earthman, so shut
your face or I'll kick you in the zatch.

434
00:32:38,514 --> 00:32:40,027
- You wanna fight?
- OK.

435
00:32:40,114 --> 00:32:42,788
- No.
- I'm just kidding.

436
00:32:42,875 --> 00:32:46,187
I'm a kidder. Let's be friends.
Let's connect. You and I.

437
00:32:47,037 --> 00:32:49,029
Didn't see that one coming, did you?

438
00:32:49,118 --> 00:32:52,316
Popped right out of the box.
You foxy, yeah.

439
00:32:52,679 --> 00:32:55,717
Teach your pal a lesson, Ford.
He's a guest on my ship.

440
00:32:55,840 --> 00:32:58,116
He's a guest on my ship.

441
00:32:59,082 --> 00:33:01,038
I thought you said you stole it.

442
00:33:02,603 --> 00:33:05,357
Stole what?
What are we talking about?

443
00:33:06,925 --> 00:33:08,961
That doesn't sound good.

444
00:33:13,247 --> 00:33:16,479
- Are you OK?
- Just fabulous, thanks, Trill.

445
00:33:16,568 --> 00:33:19,208
A little help over here.
I'm in over my head.

446
00:33:19,329 --> 00:33:22,562
I'll take care of this. Good Zarquon,
do I have to do everything?

447
00:33:22,691 --> 00:33:23,840
Yes, I do.

448
00:33:23,931 --> 00:33:26,048
Hey, this is really pretty.

449
00:33:26,132 --> 00:33:28,567
- Don't.
- They're on our tail.

450
00:33:28,693 --> 00:33:30,969
Fire a gun. Launch a missile.
Do some damage.

451
00:33:40,097 --> 00:33:41,087
Stop that.

452
00:33:41,178 --> 00:33:43,135
Just kiddin'. I love it rough. Hit me.

453
00:33:43,219 --> 00:33:46,018
<i>- Computer?
- Hi, there.</i>

454
00:33:46,100 --> 00:33:48,092
<i>I'm Eddie, your computer.</i>

455
00:33:48,181 --> 00:33:51,777
<i>I'm pleased to report that it's a fleet
of 100 Vogon battle destroyers.</i>

456
00:33:51,862 --> 00:33:54,058
<i>They're sending you a message.</i>

457
00:33:54,143 --> 00:33:56,419
<i>This is Vice-President Questular Rontok.</i>

458
00:33:56,504 --> 00:33:59,781
<i>I am speaking to the kidnapper
of the President?</i>

459
00:33:59,905 --> 00:34:01,021
She digs me.

460
00:34:01,106 --> 00:34:03,176
<i>Surrender the stolen vessel at once</i>

461
00:34:03,306 --> 00:34:06,664
<i>or we will take action
as defined and permitted by...</i>

462
00:34:08,628 --> 00:34:12,418
<i>section 1-8 of the Galactic Interstellar
Space Bylaws...</i>

463
00:34:12,510 --> 00:34:15,742
<i>Zaphod, please come back now.
This is ridiculous.</i>

464
00:34:15,831 --> 00:34:18,471
Leap to hyperspace. Come on.

465
00:34:18,592 --> 00:34:20,504
<i>Sure thing, fella.</i>

466
00:34:27,396 --> 00:34:28,749
No.

467
00:34:28,876 --> 00:34:34,032
Did they have proper
hyperspace authorisation?

468
00:34:34,118 --> 00:34:35,916
No, commander.

469
00:34:38,720 --> 00:34:43,113
Bring me the request
to pursue fugitive forms.

470
00:34:43,202 --> 00:34:45,114
I'm coming, sir.

471
00:34:47,564 --> 00:34:49,794
I'm boiling. Here's the form, sir.

472
00:34:53,126 --> 00:34:57,200
So are you along for the ride
or am I just dropping you somewhere?

473
00:34:57,287 --> 00:35:00,486
That depends. Where are you going?

474
00:35:00,569 --> 00:35:02,480
Where am I going?

475
00:35:03,530 --> 00:35:07,809
- You OK?
- Yeah. Don't suppose there's any tea.

476
00:35:07,891 --> 00:35:10,851
Yes, there is. Come on.
I'll show you the kitchen.

477
00:35:10,933 --> 00:35:15,246
When you see what I'm about to show you,
you'll beg me to take you with us.

478
00:35:15,334 --> 00:35:19,568
<i>The best drink in existence
is the Pan-Galactic Gargle Blaster,</i>

479
00:35:19,656 --> 00:35:24,174
<i>the effect of which is like having your
brains smashed out by a slice of lemon</i>

480
00:35:24,258 --> 00:35:26,727
<i>wrapped round a large gold brick.</i>

481
00:35:31,260 --> 00:35:34,060
- Belgium. Hold it one second.
- Belgium.

482
00:35:35,022 --> 00:35:37,776
Ape-man, Earth dude, sorry,
what's your name again?

483
00:35:37,863 --> 00:35:40,219
- Arthur.
- Right. Gorgeous.

484
00:35:40,304 --> 00:35:43,536
No hard feelings, OK?
Sorry to hear about your planet...

485
00:35:43,625 --> 00:35:45,344
- Earth.
- Yeah. I liked Earth.

486
00:35:45,426 --> 00:35:48,260
Got these boots there.
But don't mention it to the girl.

487
00:35:48,387 --> 00:35:51,984
Because if you do... I'll pull your
spleen out through your throat.

488
00:35:52,069 --> 00:35:54,868
- All right. Thanks, buddy.
- OK.

489
00:35:54,950 --> 00:35:57,863
Good stuff. Like those jammies.

490
00:36:00,552 --> 00:36:03,431
Zaphod, what's with the two-head thing?

491
00:36:04,153 --> 00:36:07,989
Oh, yeah. Apparently, you can't
be president with a whole brain.

492
00:36:08,075 --> 00:36:10,031
It's crazy.

493
00:36:10,116 --> 00:36:12,152
- Oh, so you carved it up?
- Yes.

494
00:36:12,236 --> 00:36:16,197
Parts of my personality weren't exactly
what you'd call presidential.

495
00:36:16,278 --> 00:36:18,156
You know what I'm talkin' about.

496
00:36:21,680 --> 00:36:24,149
<i>Your tea is ready.</i>

497
00:36:35,485 --> 00:36:40,163
I suppose I should've said
it resembles tea.

498
00:36:40,247 --> 00:36:42,921
So, two heads
is what does it for a girl?

499
00:36:43,008 --> 00:36:45,239
I mean, if I'd had two heads,
or three...

500
00:36:45,329 --> 00:36:47,400
Or your own spaceship.

501
00:36:48,330 --> 00:36:50,128
Anything else he's got two of?

502
00:36:50,211 --> 00:36:52,601
Come on, Arthur, don't be like that.

503
00:36:52,692 --> 00:36:56,767
What am I supposed to be like?
Green? Bleeping?

504
00:36:56,854 --> 00:36:59,528
I can fold my eyelids
inside out if you like.

505
00:36:59,615 --> 00:37:03,291
OK, look. Do you see this?

506
00:37:03,416 --> 00:37:07,297
This detects what you're craving
and makes it for you.

507
00:37:07,378 --> 00:37:09,529
<i>Enjoy your doughnut.</i>

508
00:37:13,140 --> 00:37:15,052
Do you see this?

509
00:37:18,062 --> 00:37:24,253
This toasts bread
while you're slicing it.

510
00:37:25,185 --> 00:37:29,896
We're on a spaceship, Arthur. In space.

511
00:37:29,986 --> 00:37:32,786
- I told you I wanted to get away.
- To Madagascar.

512
00:37:32,868 --> 00:37:36,179
That was some sort of test.

513
00:37:37,149 --> 00:37:38,822
And I failed.

514
00:37:38,910 --> 00:37:42,267
- How badly does it hurt?
- It doesn't feel great.

515
00:37:42,351 --> 00:37:44,308
No, I mean your chin.

516
00:37:44,392 --> 00:37:46,463
I might have an aspirin.

517
00:37:46,553 --> 00:37:47,906
Right.

518
00:37:54,276 --> 00:37:59,227
Tricia... Trillian, sorry.
There's something I have to tell you.

519
00:37:59,318 --> 00:38:02,470
- Please, don't.
- Showtime, Trill.

520
00:38:08,922 --> 00:38:11,391
I'm going to set up.
Buttons aren't toys.

521
00:38:11,483 --> 00:38:13,075
Buttons aren't toys.

522
00:38:13,163 --> 00:38:16,122
Anyway... When I saw
what I'm about to show you,

523
00:38:16,204 --> 00:38:20,677
that's when I realised
why I had to do what I did to my brain.

524
00:38:20,766 --> 00:38:23,804
I think. It's all a little...

525
00:38:24,568 --> 00:38:25,557
...shaky.

526
00:38:28,489 --> 00:38:31,482
I've seen it. It's rubbish.

527
00:38:34,932 --> 00:38:37,048
<i>Many millions of years ago,</i>

528
00:38:37,132 --> 00:38:40,444
<i>a race of hyperintelligent,
pan-dimensional beings</i>

529
00:38:40,534 --> 00:38:44,289
<i>got so fed up with the constant
bickering about the meaning of life,</i>

530
00:38:44,375 --> 00:38:47,209
<i>that they commissioned
two of their brightest and best</i>

531
00:38:47,336 --> 00:38:50,489
<i>to design and build
a stupendous super-computer</i>

532
00:38:50,578 --> 00:38:54,777
<i>to calculate the answer to life,
the universe and everything.</i>

533
00:38:54,859 --> 00:38:57,738
O Deep Thought,

534
00:38:57,821 --> 00:39:01,895
we want you to tell us the answer.

535
00:39:02,022 --> 00:39:04,139
<i>The answer to what?</i>

536
00:39:04,223 --> 00:39:08,662
The answer to life,
the universe, everything.

537
00:39:08,785 --> 00:39:12,620
We'd really like an answer.
Something simple.

538
00:39:12,706 --> 00:39:15,267
<i>I'd have to think about that.</i>

539
00:39:15,347 --> 00:39:20,582
<i>Return to this place in exactly
seven-and-a-half million years.</i>

540
00:39:22,030 --> 00:39:25,580
- Is it finished?
- No, there's more. They go back.

541
00:39:25,671 --> 00:39:30,542
- Seven-and-a-half million years later?
- That's right, they do.

542
00:39:46,559 --> 00:39:48,949
Deep Thought, do you have...

543
00:39:49,040 --> 00:39:53,274
<i>An answer for you?
Yes, but you're not going to like it.</i>

544
00:39:54,402 --> 00:39:57,362
<i>- It doesn't matter. We must know it.
- All right.</i>

545
00:39:57,444 --> 00:40:00,243
<i>The answer to the ultimate question</i>

546
00:40:02,125 --> 00:40:05,597
<i>of life, the universe
and everything...</i>

547
00:40:07,207 --> 00:40:08,243
<i>... is...</i>

548
00:40:14,810 --> 00:40:16,290
<i>... 42.</i>

549
00:40:16,371 --> 00:40:18,203
Yeah? What?

550
00:40:19,772 --> 00:40:21,570
42?

551
00:40:21,653 --> 00:40:25,648
<i>Yes, I thought it over quite thoroughly.
It's 42.</i>

552
00:40:25,735 --> 00:40:28,887
<i>It would've been simpler
to have known what the question was.</i>

553
00:40:28,976 --> 00:40:33,654
But it was the question.
The ultimate question. Of everything.

554
00:40:33,738 --> 00:40:35,535
<i>That's not a question.</i>

555
00:40:35,658 --> 00:40:39,209
<i>Only when you know the question
will you know what the answer means.</i>

556
00:40:39,300 --> 00:40:43,739
<i>- Give us the ultimate question then.
- I can't. But there is one who can.</i>

557
00:40:43,822 --> 00:40:47,611
<i>A computer that will calculate
the ultimate question.</i>

558
00:40:47,703 --> 00:40:50,172
<i>A computer of such infinite complexity,</i>

559
00:40:50,304 --> 00:40:54,185
<i>that life itself will form part
of its operational matrix.</i>

560
00:40:54,346 --> 00:40:58,260
<i>And you yourselves shall take on new,
more primitive forms,</i>

561
00:40:58,387 --> 00:41:03,861
<i>and go down into the computer
to navigate its 10-million-year program.</i>

562
00:41:03,989 --> 00:41:07,540
<i>I shall design this computer for you,
and it shall be called...</i>

563
00:41:12,833 --> 00:41:16,224
- That's it?
- That's it.

564
00:41:16,314 --> 00:41:20,025
You're looking for
the ultimate question? You? Why?

565
00:41:20,116 --> 00:41:22,790
I tried that.
Why? 42. Doesn't work.

566
00:41:22,877 --> 00:41:25,631
- Let's get it ready, baby.
- Why...

567
00:41:25,718 --> 00:41:28,711
...do you want to know
the ultimate question?

568
00:41:28,799 --> 00:41:31,633
Partly curiosity,
partly a sense of adventure,

569
00:41:31,800 --> 00:41:35,032
but mostly I think
it's for the fame and the money.

570
00:41:35,121 --> 00:41:37,192
You're President of the Galaxy.

571
00:41:37,282 --> 00:41:39,080
- Yes, Arman.
- Arthur.

572
00:41:39,163 --> 00:41:41,439
Whatever.
Presidential fame is temporary.

573
00:41:41,524 --> 00:41:45,154
I find the question, that's permanent.
It sticks.

574
00:41:45,285 --> 00:41:47,675
Plus, everyone thinks you're deep.
Win-win.

575
00:41:48,367 --> 00:41:52,839
We just hit that button and bam,
we're at Magrathea, I think.

576
00:41:52,928 --> 00:41:56,002
We've hit it twice and
we're still not there, but anyway...

577
00:41:56,130 --> 00:41:58,439
- You in?
- Always.

578
00:41:58,531 --> 00:42:01,205
- All right.
- I want to get off.

579
00:42:01,292 --> 00:42:04,603
- Sorry, what exactly are we doing?
- This.

580
00:42:08,654 --> 00:42:12,205
<i>The Infinite Improbability Drive
is a wonderful new method</i>

581
00:42:12,296 --> 00:42:15,016
<i>of crossing interstellar distances
in a few seconds,</i>

582
00:42:15,097 --> 00:42:18,135
<i>without all that tedious
mucking about in hyperspace.</i>

583
00:42:18,218 --> 00:42:21,336
<i>As the Improbability Drive
reaches infinite improbability,</i>

584
00:42:21,419 --> 00:42:25,255
<i>it passes through every conceivable
point in every conceivable universe</i>

585
00:42:25,341 --> 00:42:27,014
<i>almost simultaneously.</i>

586
00:42:27,102 --> 00:42:29,093
<i>So you're never sure
where you'll end up</i>

587
00:42:29,182 --> 00:42:31,697
<i>or even what species you'll be
when you get there.</i>

588
00:42:31,783 --> 00:42:34,618
<i>It's therefore important
to dress accordingly.</i>

589
00:42:34,705 --> 00:42:38,858
<i>The Drive was invented following
research into finite improbability</i>

590
00:42:39,026 --> 00:42:40,983
<i>often used to break the ice at parties</i>

591
00:42:41,067 --> 00:42:44,185
<i>by making all the molecules
in the hostess's undergarments</i>

592
00:42:44,268 --> 00:42:46,225
<i>leap one foot to the left</i>

593
00:42:46,309 --> 00:42:48,744
<i>in accordance with
the theory of indeterminacy.</i>

594
00:42:48,870 --> 00:42:52,261
<i>Many physicists said they
wouldn't stand for that sort of thing,</i>

595
00:42:52,351 --> 00:42:54,388
<i>partly because it debased science,</i>

596
00:42:54,472 --> 00:42:58,308
<i>but mostly because they didn't get
invited to those sort of parties.</i>

597
00:43:08,117 --> 00:43:11,429
Is this gonna happen
every time we hit that button?

598
00:43:11,519 --> 00:43:13,351
Very probably, yes.

599
00:43:13,480 --> 00:43:16,314
Ah, I think
the Earthman's about to be sick.

600
00:43:16,401 --> 00:43:19,155
In the trashcan, ape-man.
This ship's new.

601
00:43:20,042 --> 00:43:21,761
Come on.

602
00:43:21,883 --> 00:43:24,273
We have normality.

603
00:43:24,404 --> 00:43:25,917
Did it work? Are we there?

604
00:43:27,725 --> 00:43:31,720
Yeah. We're here. Magrathea.

605
00:43:31,807 --> 00:43:33,445
I don't think so. Eddie...

606
00:43:33,567 --> 00:43:35,445
What planet are we looking at?

607
00:43:35,568 --> 00:43:37,287
<i>I'm checking for you.</i>

608
00:43:37,369 --> 00:43:42,161
Did you just pluck
one of my hairs? Off my head?

609
00:43:42,931 --> 00:43:45,651
<i>Thank you so much for waiting.
Sorry to disappoint,</i>

610
00:43:45,732 --> 00:43:47,724
<i>but this is not Magrathea.</i>

611
00:43:47,853 --> 00:43:51,449
<i>We are currently in orbit around
the planet Viltvodle Six.</i>

612
00:43:52,415 --> 00:43:54,326
Humma Kavula.

613
00:43:55,216 --> 00:43:59,928
Magrathea's gonna have to wait.
I got a score to settle on this planet.

614
00:44:00,738 --> 00:44:03,537
Humma Kavula.

615
00:44:07,621 --> 00:44:11,661
<i>In the beginning,
the universe was created.</i>

616
00:44:11,782 --> 00:44:16,380
<i>This made a lot of people very angry,
and is widely regarded as a bad move.</i>

617
00:44:18,425 --> 00:44:21,577
<i>Many races believed it was created
by some sort of god,</i>

618
00:44:21,706 --> 00:44:24,141
<i>though the Jatravartid people
of Viltvodle Six</i>

619
00:44:24,227 --> 00:44:28,984
<i>firmly believe that the entire universe
was sneezed out of the nose of a being</i>

620
00:44:29,069 --> 00:44:31,743
<i>called the Great Green Arkleseizure.</i>

621
00:44:31,830 --> 00:44:33,423
Humma Kavula.

622
00:44:33,511 --> 00:44:35,741
<i>The Jatravartids, who lived in fear</i>

623
00:44:35,831 --> 00:44:39,189
<i>of the time they called the
Coming of the Great White Handkerchief,</i>

624
00:44:39,273 --> 00:44:42,869
<i>were small blue creatures
with more than 50 arms each.</i>

625
00:44:42,954 --> 00:44:45,389
<i>They were unique in being
the only race in history</i>

626
00:44:45,475 --> 00:44:49,072
<i>to have invented
the aerosol deodorant before the wheel.</i>

627
00:44:50,917 --> 00:44:52,829
Mr President.

628
00:44:52,918 --> 00:44:54,557
Hey, all right. How you doin'?

629
00:44:54,679 --> 00:44:56,476
What have we here? I love it.

630
00:45:01,882 --> 00:45:03,918
Hey, I love you. Fantastic.

631
00:45:04,002 --> 00:45:06,517
You guys related? Don't go changing.

632
00:45:06,603 --> 00:45:08,595
Arthur, I want a drink.

633
00:45:08,684 --> 00:45:12,679
I think I've been here before.
Have I been here before?

634
00:45:16,167 --> 00:45:18,124
Lxxie.

635
00:45:18,248 --> 00:45:21,082
- I've been here before.
- Humma Kavula.

636
00:45:21,169 --> 00:45:22,842
Come on.

637
00:45:22,930 --> 00:45:25,650
Tricia, I...

638
00:45:30,413 --> 00:45:38,209
<i>Now we await
with eager expectation</i>

639
00:45:38,336 --> 00:45:44,607
<i>Thy handkerchief
to bring us back to Thee</i>

640
00:45:53,061 --> 00:45:54,814
Hello, Humma.

641
00:45:56,263 --> 00:46:00,462
- Join us. Sit down.
- Good to see you. Thanks. All right.

642
00:46:00,544 --> 00:46:02,581
The handkerchief is coming.

643
00:46:02,665 --> 00:46:08,299
Let us pray the Almighty will exhale
a breath of compassion upon us all.

644
00:46:09,908 --> 00:46:13,663
So that's Humma Kavula.
I thought he was just swearing.

645
00:46:14,390 --> 00:46:18,509
We lift our noses, clogged and unblown,

646
00:46:18,591 --> 00:46:20,628
in reverence to you.

647
00:46:22,313 --> 00:46:27,025
Send the handkerchief, O Blessed One,
so that it may wipe us clean.

648
00:46:27,115 --> 00:46:32,589
We ask this and all things
in Thy precious and alliterative name.

649
00:46:41,160 --> 00:46:42,958
Bless you.

650
00:46:43,401 --> 00:46:45,632
We goin' in here?

651
00:46:45,722 --> 00:46:49,398
All right, I like it. It's big,
it's gold, it's fancy. Fancy pants.

652
00:46:49,483 --> 00:46:52,875
Zaphod Beeblebrox,
our infamous President.

653
00:46:52,965 --> 00:46:55,605
What brings you to our humble planet?

654
00:46:55,686 --> 00:46:59,646
- I think you know why I'm here.
- No, I don't think I do.

655
00:46:59,727 --> 00:47:01,366
I think you think you don't.

656
00:47:02,408 --> 00:47:05,128
But we both know you do.

657
00:47:05,930 --> 00:47:07,727
Eloquent as always.

658
00:47:07,810 --> 00:47:11,521
Your ability to articulate
never ceases to amaze.

659
00:47:11,612 --> 00:47:14,525
That's funny.
During the campaign, Humma,

660
00:47:14,613 --> 00:47:16,923
when you were my opponent
running against me,

661
00:47:17,014 --> 00:47:18,926
you said I was stupid.

662
00:47:37,782 --> 00:47:42,016
The election is ancient history,
Zaphod, but...

663
00:47:44,385 --> 00:47:46,182
...if memory serves, you won,

664
00:47:46,265 --> 00:47:48,860
proving that good looks
and charm

665
00:47:48,946 --> 00:47:54,227
win over brilliance
and the ability to govern.

666
00:47:58,310 --> 00:48:01,428
And, incidentally, you are stupid.

667
00:48:01,511 --> 00:48:03,980
Excuse me, Mr Humma, sir.

668
00:48:05,073 --> 00:48:08,066
I just want to say, there's been
some sort of terrible mix up

669
00:48:08,154 --> 00:48:10,464
cos, actually, he's not with us.

670
00:48:10,555 --> 00:48:13,354
We came to worship you.
He followed us and...

671
00:48:13,436 --> 00:48:16,076
He grew, didn't he. He's not that tall.

672
00:48:16,197 --> 00:48:20,989
You didn't come half-way across the
galaxy to settle a campaign grudge.

673
00:48:22,439 --> 00:48:24,396
Why are you here?

674
00:48:25,921 --> 00:48:28,482
Of course not, that's ridiculous.

675
00:48:28,562 --> 00:48:33,957
I've been stranded on a strange planet
for years, I haven't been avoiding you.

676
00:48:34,044 --> 00:48:36,923
You look great. You're doing well.

677
00:48:37,005 --> 00:48:38,644
You've grown, obviously.

678
00:48:39,486 --> 00:48:42,604
Wait. We don't know why we're here.

679
00:48:42,687 --> 00:48:46,158
We were trying to get to Magrathea
and our ship brought us here.

680
00:48:47,689 --> 00:48:51,046
How very, very improbable.

681
00:48:58,173 --> 00:49:00,847
I kept a few souvenirs
from my former life.

682
00:49:00,934 --> 00:49:03,733
Ah, the heady days of space piracy.

683
00:49:04,696 --> 00:49:11,171
But even an Infinite lmprobability Drive
requires coordinates.

684
00:49:11,298 --> 00:49:13,574
Which I happen to have.

685
00:49:13,699 --> 00:49:15,258
No, no, no.

686
00:49:15,380 --> 00:49:17,656
You don't get something
for nothing, Zaphod.

687
00:49:17,781 --> 00:49:20,296
You must bring me something in return.

688
00:49:20,382 --> 00:49:23,181
- What?
- A gun.

689
00:49:23,263 --> 00:49:25,141
- A gun?
- A very special gun.

690
00:49:25,224 --> 00:49:28,422
Designed by the greatest computer
ever invented.

691
00:49:28,505 --> 00:49:31,304
But the only way to find it
is to go to Magrathea.

692
00:49:31,386 --> 00:49:35,700
Fine. I'll get your gun.
Just give me the coordinates.

693
00:49:35,788 --> 00:49:38,462
And what will you give me
to ensure your return?

694
00:49:39,269 --> 00:49:41,579
My word as President.

695
00:49:42,830 --> 00:49:46,188
No. I need a hostage.

696
00:49:48,593 --> 00:49:55,512
Only, what does
Zaphod Beeblebrox treasure?

697
00:49:59,157 --> 00:50:01,069
Come on.

698
00:50:03,959 --> 00:50:05,711
Oh, no.

699
00:50:05,839 --> 00:50:08,513
Hey, take it easy now. Think about this.

700
00:50:08,600 --> 00:50:11,639
No, stop it.
That kind of tickles.

701
00:50:11,722 --> 00:50:15,159
Two heads are better than one.
Double your pleasure.

702
00:50:15,923 --> 00:50:18,836
It's not fair. You need me.

703
00:50:18,964 --> 00:50:22,959
Come back. Don't leave me alone.

704
00:50:25,886 --> 00:50:27,798
Come back.

705
00:50:27,927 --> 00:50:31,557
I thought I alone
considered your boyfriend

706
00:50:31,648 --> 00:50:34,209
a narcissistic moron,
but the whole galaxy does.

707
00:50:34,289 --> 00:50:37,009
What about you?
"Excuse me, Mr Humma, sir."

708
00:50:37,090 --> 00:50:41,370
"We're not with him. We just came
to worship you." Very brave.

709
00:50:41,452 --> 00:50:43,364
Where the hell is Ford?

710
00:50:47,214 --> 00:50:53,406
By the left, march.
Left, right, left, right.

711
00:50:53,497 --> 00:50:57,616
Mr President,
we're here for your protection.

712
00:50:58,059 --> 00:51:00,574
Fire upon the kidnapper.

713
00:51:01,300 --> 00:51:02,574
<i>Hey, come on.</i>

714
00:51:02,660 --> 00:51:05,129
The President is the kidnapper.
You'll kill him.

715
00:51:07,142 --> 00:51:09,020
It's locked.

716
00:51:11,824 --> 00:51:14,100
Thank you, darling. I'll be in touch.

717
00:51:19,187 --> 00:51:21,543
Come on, Zaphod, man.

718
00:51:24,909 --> 00:51:27,549
- Hey, what did I miss?
- What have you been doing?

719
00:51:27,630 --> 00:51:30,987
Field research. So they found
where we are? Belgium bummer.

720
00:51:31,071 --> 00:51:33,711
They're shooting at us.
What are we going to do?

721
00:51:33,832 --> 00:51:36,951
- I have an idea.
- Pushing him out and running away?

722
00:51:37,034 --> 00:51:39,424
What? He's the one they want.

723
00:51:39,515 --> 00:51:41,665
You can't go out there.
What are you doing?

724
00:51:42,916 --> 00:51:45,750
- Back off.
- What's going on?

725
00:51:47,358 --> 00:51:50,510
I have the President
and I will kill him, I swear.

726
00:51:50,599 --> 00:51:54,388
- Could that actually kill him?
- I don't think so.

727
00:51:54,520 --> 00:51:56,591
It's an aerosol can.

728
00:51:56,681 --> 00:51:59,071
OK, frightened now, frightened.

729
00:52:00,643 --> 00:52:03,954
- Hi, little guy.
- Get her.

730
00:52:04,044 --> 00:52:06,923
- Zaphod, no, come on.
- Arthur.

731
00:52:07,686 --> 00:52:10,406
- Arthur.
- Tricia.

732
00:52:10,487 --> 00:52:13,366
- Arthur.
- Tricia. Wait.

733
00:52:21,971 --> 00:52:24,361
Arthur.

734
00:52:27,013 --> 00:52:29,892
Resistance is useless.

735
00:52:39,458 --> 00:52:43,054
<i>Bring her to Vogsphere
for processing, captain.</i>

736
00:52:44,740 --> 00:52:46,413
Where's the circus?

737
00:52:46,501 --> 00:52:49,062
They got her and it's my fault.
We have to go.

738
00:52:49,142 --> 00:52:50,541
Let's go.

739
00:52:53,984 --> 00:52:55,895
Computer, take us to Magrathea.

740
00:52:55,984 --> 00:52:58,374
What? No.
We've got to go after Tricia.

741
00:52:58,465 --> 00:52:59,785
- Who?
- Trillian.

742
00:52:59,866 --> 00:53:03,257
They've got Trillian,
you stupid half-brained git.

743
00:53:03,347 --> 00:53:05,623
Right, you're coming out.

744
00:53:06,388 --> 00:53:10,144
Computer, we don't want to go to
Magrathea, we want to follow the ships.

745
00:53:10,230 --> 00:53:13,746
<i>I'd love to, but my guidance system
has been deactivated.</i>

746
00:53:13,871 --> 00:53:14,907
Oh, come on.

747
00:53:15,032 --> 00:53:17,308
<i>I'm sorry. It wasn't my fault.</i>

748
00:53:17,393 --> 00:53:19,349
What you doing on this ship?

749
00:53:19,473 --> 00:53:20,987
- Ford?
- Marvin.

750
00:53:21,074 --> 00:53:23,384
I've been talking
to the ship's computer.

751
00:53:23,515 --> 00:53:25,108
And?

752
00:53:25,196 --> 00:53:26,675
It hates me.

753
00:53:27,036 --> 00:53:29,835
No. Eddie, computer,

754
00:53:29,917 --> 00:53:33,275
is there another way
to follow the ships?

755
00:53:33,399 --> 00:53:37,678
<i>Activating Emergency Escape Pod.
It's super-neat and fun to fly.</i>

756
00:53:40,001 --> 00:53:41,435
Unbelievable.

757
00:53:41,522 --> 00:53:45,642
I'm in. OK, Ford,
do you have any idea?

758
00:53:45,724 --> 00:53:47,919
- We'll press this button.
- No.

759
00:53:49,485 --> 00:53:52,364
- This button.
- OK. Nothing's... OK.

760
00:53:52,486 --> 00:53:55,320
Good man.

761
00:53:55,407 --> 00:53:58,845
- OK, we're wobbling...
- You forgot your towel.

762
00:53:58,929 --> 00:54:01,319
Get him off me. I'll kill him.

763
00:54:01,410 --> 00:54:03,800
- OK, Ford, there's a...
- Is that better?

764
00:54:03,891 --> 00:54:05,847
No, that's worse.

765
00:54:05,971 --> 00:54:09,090
- Do what... I don't need that.
- OK.

766
00:54:09,173 --> 00:54:11,813
Stop the rocking.
Marvin, any ideas?

767
00:54:11,894 --> 00:54:15,365
I have a million ideas.
They all point to certain death.

768
00:54:15,455 --> 00:54:16,809
Thanks, Marv.

769
00:55:01,073 --> 00:55:04,953
- Magrathea.
- No, we're on the Vogon planet.

770
00:55:05,034 --> 00:55:07,549
- Yes, it is. It's Magrathea.
- This is Vogsphere.

771
00:55:07,635 --> 00:55:10,673
- Yeah, Magrathea. I know it.
- No, it's not.

772
00:55:10,756 --> 00:55:14,148
- There is no Magrathea.
- Yes, it is.

773
00:55:15,238 --> 00:55:17,309
- What are you doing?
- They took my head.

774
00:55:17,399 --> 00:55:20,119
They have these
on Arcturan megafreighters.

775
00:55:20,200 --> 00:55:22,635
They use them
when they need to concentrate.

776
00:55:22,721 --> 00:55:24,872
- What is it?
- It's a thinking cap.

777
00:55:29,444 --> 00:55:31,481
There you go.

778
00:55:35,086 --> 00:55:38,966
That should give him some zest
for about ten minutes or so.

779
00:55:39,047 --> 00:55:42,359
Now I'm feeling much better
about our predicament.

780
00:56:02,817 --> 00:56:06,811
I checked the Guide for how
to rescue a prisoner from Vogsphere.

781
00:56:06,898 --> 00:56:09,891
It said don't.
I'm assuming you have a better plan.

782
00:56:09,979 --> 00:56:12,448
Well, I kind of had this idea
that we could...

783
00:56:14,421 --> 00:56:16,538
- Did anyone see that?
- See what?

784
00:56:16,662 --> 00:56:18,460
What was it?

785
00:56:18,583 --> 00:56:22,702
Nothing, it's nothing.
It's just my imagination.

786
00:56:22,824 --> 00:56:26,865
OK, right. Now, everyone just stop
a minute, please, and look at me.

787
00:56:26,946 --> 00:56:31,658
There's definitely something
going on here.

788
00:56:33,349 --> 00:56:35,499
Just watch.

789
00:56:35,589 --> 00:56:37,501
Just watch.

790
00:56:40,791 --> 00:56:43,147
OK, I think we...

791
00:56:44,473 --> 00:56:45,872
You too.

792
00:56:46,874 --> 00:56:49,150
Yeah, what was that?

793
00:56:52,396 --> 00:56:54,308
Zaphod, what do you think?

794
00:56:57,478 --> 00:57:01,154
I think... Zarquon.
What was that?

795
00:57:02,120 --> 00:57:05,557
I'd make a suggestion,
but you wouldn't listen.

796
00:57:05,681 --> 00:57:08,480
- No one ever does.
- I have an idea.

797
00:57:08,562 --> 00:57:11,555
Ford. Kill it. Get rid of it.

798
00:57:19,606 --> 00:57:22,326
OK, don't think.

799
00:57:22,408 --> 00:57:24,797
Nobody think. No ideas.

800
00:57:24,888 --> 00:57:26,959
No theories. No nothing.

801
00:57:32,892 --> 00:57:35,407
This is crazy.
Trillian is in there somewhere.

802
00:57:35,493 --> 00:57:40,124
- We need an idea to help save her.
- I have an...

803
00:57:40,214 --> 00:57:42,251
Run.

804
00:58:10,426 --> 00:58:12,463
You're supposed to stop.

805
00:58:21,310 --> 00:58:25,430
OK, so I'm not thinking.
Not thinking. No ideas coming.

806
00:58:28,153 --> 00:58:31,146
OK, this isn't an idea
that has any merit at all,

807
00:58:31,274 --> 00:58:34,951
but, Marvin, can you give me a hand?

808
00:58:37,317 --> 00:58:39,387
OK, where is she?

809
00:58:42,238 --> 00:58:44,958
Who, the director of robot arm repair?

810
00:58:46,560 --> 00:58:50,919
Tricia Yggarstuk McMillanus of Blaard?

811
00:58:51,002 --> 00:58:54,075
No, Tricia Marie McMillan of Earth.

812
00:58:54,203 --> 00:58:55,717
Oh. Right.

813
00:59:01,326 --> 00:59:04,603
Sorry, no record of Earth.

814
00:59:05,367 --> 00:59:08,998
Galactic sector
ZZ9 Plural Z Alpha?

815
00:59:09,089 --> 00:59:12,400
- Oh, yes.
- She's lying.

816
00:59:13,851 --> 00:59:17,049
She's skinny, and she's pretty,
and she's lying.

817
00:59:18,292 --> 00:59:20,761
Oh, yes, here we are. Earth.

818
00:59:22,694 --> 00:59:24,094
"Destroyed."

819
00:59:24,215 --> 00:59:27,413
Do you have
a second home planet?

820
00:59:27,496 --> 00:59:30,933
- Destroyed? That's impossible.
- That's what it says here.

821
00:59:31,057 --> 00:59:36,008
Says it was destroyed to make way
for a hyperspace expressway.

822
00:59:42,782 --> 00:59:46,219
Who in their right mind
gives an order to destroy a planet?

823
00:59:48,264 --> 00:59:52,019
- He said the gray building, right?
- All the buildings are gray.

824
00:59:52,106 --> 00:59:55,383
"Give me a hand." Very funny.

825
00:59:55,467 --> 00:59:58,778
How am I supposed to drive this pod
with one arm? Stupid human.

826
01:00:03,030 --> 01:00:06,068
So, you need to go back
and fill that in.

827
01:00:06,151 --> 01:00:09,349
Then, make sure, when you fill in
the facilitating form...

828
01:00:07,872 --> 01:00:12,549
Leave this to me. I'm British.
I know how to queue.

829
01:00:14,114 --> 01:00:19,031
If you'd fill that form in for me,
and return it as soon as possible.

830
01:00:19,116 --> 01:00:23,270
- Writing implements to your left...
- You're President. Can you do anything?

831
01:00:23,358 --> 01:00:28,035
Presidents don't have power. Their job
is to draw attention away from it.

832
01:00:30,040 --> 01:00:31,997
Hey, how you doin'?

833
01:00:32,081 --> 01:00:36,599
Hey, it's me, your President.
What's goin' on? Thanks for coming.

834
01:00:36,683 --> 01:00:39,403
All right. I know that sponge.
All right.

835
01:00:39,524 --> 01:00:41,800
- Next.
- President coming through.

836
01:00:41,925 --> 01:00:44,759
How you doin', Pinhead?
No, really, it's me.

837
01:00:45,606 --> 01:00:47,325
I love kabuki. Look at that.

838
01:00:47,407 --> 01:00:49,319
Out the way.

839
01:00:51,008 --> 01:00:53,204
Give us a kiss.

840
01:00:54,210 --> 01:00:56,964
Hi, I've come about
release of a prisoner.

841
01:00:57,051 --> 01:00:59,087
Prisoner release form.

842
01:01:02,333 --> 01:01:04,450
I don't believe you.

843
01:01:04,534 --> 01:01:06,411
These are the orders.

844
01:01:11,296 --> 01:01:13,208
"Love and kisses"?

845
01:01:13,977 --> 01:01:17,335
Now, according
to the Galactic Penal Code,

846
01:01:17,419 --> 01:01:21,936
the punishment for
a presidential kidnapping

847
01:01:22,020 --> 01:01:25,617
is to be fed to
the Ravenous Bugblatter Beast of Traal.

848
01:01:26,382 --> 01:01:28,374
Good day.

849
01:01:29,983 --> 01:01:34,137
Get off of me. What the hell is that?

850
01:01:35,466 --> 01:01:39,540
No, wait.
There's been some kind of mistake.

851
01:01:41,948 --> 01:01:47,308
- Tick all the boxes on the right.
- Right. Got it.

852
01:01:47,390 --> 01:01:49,302
Not that one.

853
01:01:49,391 --> 01:01:53,511
<i>What to do if you find yourself
stuck with no hope of rescue.</i>

854
01:01:53,593 --> 01:01:57,792
<i>Consider how lucky you are
that life has been good to you so far.</i>

855
01:01:57,874 --> 01:02:00,867
<i>Alternatively, if life
hasn't been good to you so far,</i>

856
01:02:00,955 --> 01:02:04,188
<i>which, given your current circumstances,
seems more likely,</i>

857
01:02:04,277 --> 01:02:08,317
<i>consider how lucky you are that
it won't be troubling you much longer.</i>

858
01:02:11,600 --> 01:02:14,399
Right, there we go.

859
01:02:14,481 --> 01:02:16,757
Kidnapping the President.

860
01:02:16,882 --> 01:02:21,115
Oh, no, she's not eligible
for release at this time.

861
01:02:21,203 --> 01:02:24,241
OK. Right.
Look, this is the President.

862
01:02:24,324 --> 01:02:26,361
OK. See, there?

863
01:02:26,445 --> 01:02:29,917
He says the kidnapping business
was a horrible misunderstanding.

864
01:02:30,007 --> 01:02:31,520
Oh, yeah.

865
01:02:31,607 --> 01:02:35,158
She meant nothing by it.
He's ordering you to let her go.

866
01:02:35,249 --> 01:02:39,801
But this isn't a presidential
release of prisoner form.

867
01:02:39,891 --> 01:02:41,802
Those are blue.

868
01:02:43,052 --> 01:02:46,966
- I'll stay here.
- All right. Hold that. OK.

869
01:02:51,455 --> 01:02:53,686
Oh, he's hungry today.

870
01:02:56,177 --> 01:02:57,656
That's blue. Right.

871
01:02:59,818 --> 01:03:02,617
- I'm not a journalist.
- We're just hanging out.

872
01:03:02,699 --> 01:03:05,168
OK, this is for Tricia McMillan.

873
01:03:05,941 --> 01:03:09,173
Tricia McMillan. OK.

874
01:03:11,223 --> 01:03:15,183
Sir, it's a release form.

875
01:03:15,304 --> 01:03:16,499
Thank you.

876
01:03:16,585 --> 01:03:19,623
She's free to go.

877
01:03:19,706 --> 01:03:21,698
Release her.

878
01:03:24,388 --> 01:03:28,064
Zaphod's here.
Well, then, that's fine then.

879
01:03:28,149 --> 01:03:30,061
Let's just go and get him.

880
01:03:37,833 --> 01:03:40,428
Who are we waiting for again?

881
01:03:44,315 --> 01:03:46,227
No, I'm serious.

882
01:03:48,117 --> 01:03:50,313
Tricia.

883
01:03:53,239 --> 01:03:55,151
- Tricia.
- Trill.

884
01:03:55,680 --> 01:03:58,479
- Hey, come on.
- You idiot.

885
01:03:58,561 --> 01:04:00,518
You signed the order to destroy Earth.

886
01:04:00,602 --> 01:04:02,354
- He did?
- I did?

887
01:04:02,443 --> 01:04:06,722
Yes. "Love and kisses, Zaphod"?
You didn't even read it, did you?

888
01:04:06,804 --> 01:04:11,402
Honey, I'm President of the Galaxy.
I don't get a lot of time for reading.

889
01:04:13,407 --> 01:04:15,159
An entire planet, my home,

890
01:04:15,247 --> 01:04:18,446
destroyed because you thought
they wanted your autograph.

891
01:04:18,529 --> 01:04:20,998
- They framed me.
- Are you an idiot?

892
01:04:21,090 --> 01:04:24,845
- Trillian.
- You knew. Why didn't you tell me?

893
01:04:24,931 --> 01:04:28,812
- He threatened me.
- Get a backbone, Arthur.

894
01:04:28,893 --> 01:04:33,844
Oh, a backbone? What about coming here
to rescue you? Thank you. My idea.

895
01:04:35,055 --> 01:04:36,967
Stupid.

896
01:04:41,097 --> 01:04:43,328
- Got your arm.
- How considerate.

897
01:04:43,418 --> 01:04:47,618
Right, then, all those lucky enough
to have two arms, hold tight.

898
01:05:03,626 --> 01:05:06,619
The President tests my patience.

899
01:05:06,707 --> 01:05:11,339
This time I shall pursue him myself.
Ready my ship.

900
01:05:11,429 --> 01:05:14,228
Fantastic. At last.

901
01:05:19,312 --> 01:05:22,511
That's one hour for lunch, everybody.

902
01:05:23,274 --> 01:05:25,345
I think I'll have soup today.

903
01:05:27,355 --> 01:05:30,713
<i>The Encyclopaedia Galactica,
in its chapter on love,</i>

904
01:05:30,797 --> 01:05:34,074
<i>states that it is
far too complicated to define.</i>

905
01:05:34,158 --> 01:05:38,392
<i>The Hitchhiker's Guide
to the Galaxy has this to say on love:</i>

906
01:05:38,560 --> 01:05:40,995
<i>"Avoid, if at all possible."</i>

907
01:05:41,081 --> 01:05:43,835
<i>Unfortunately,
Arthur Dent has never read</i>

908
01:05:43,922 --> 01:05:46,278
The Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy.

909
01:05:46,403 --> 01:05:50,192
- Oh, I'm so sorry.
- No, come in.

910
01:05:50,324 --> 01:05:52,441
I didn't...

911
01:05:55,286 --> 01:05:56,879
Look...

912
01:05:56,967 --> 01:05:59,766
Trillian, I just wanted to say,
you were right,

913
01:05:59,848 --> 01:06:05,163
I should've told you, and I understand
totally if you're angry with me.

914
01:06:05,250 --> 01:06:08,800
For what it's worth,
I know how you must be feeling.

915
01:06:10,492 --> 01:06:14,646
- Can you hand me a towel?
- Yeah, sure, this...

916
01:06:16,054 --> 01:06:17,966
Thank you.

917
01:06:18,055 --> 01:06:20,934
Actually, what I was going to say was

918
01:06:22,177 --> 01:06:27,014
if I'd said yes to you
and gone to Madagascar,

919
01:06:27,099 --> 01:06:29,135
then neither of us would be here now.

920
01:06:30,900 --> 01:06:34,530
I just thought that might
count for something.

921
01:06:37,663 --> 01:06:39,574
It doesn't matter.

922
01:07:01,924 --> 01:07:07,319
All right, computer, take us to...
Where we headed again?

923
01:07:07,406 --> 01:07:09,841
OK, I'm gonna get you another lemon.

924
01:07:10,808 --> 01:07:12,719
All right.

925
01:07:16,850 --> 01:07:19,160
<i>Engaging Improbability Drive.</i>

926
01:07:19,251 --> 01:07:22,961
No, Zaphod, no.
Buttons are not toys. What did you do?

927
01:07:23,052 --> 01:07:24,850
<i>Magrathean coordinates accepted.</i>

928
01:07:32,376 --> 01:07:34,845
<i>It blows my circuits to tell you</i>

929
01:07:34,937 --> 01:07:38,214
<i>that we're in orbit,
at an altitude of 300 miles,</i>

930
01:07:38,338 --> 01:07:41,810
<i>around the legendary planet
of Magrathea.</i>

931
01:07:41,900 --> 01:07:44,495
Magrathea.

932
01:07:50,863 --> 01:07:52,775
Unbelievable.

933
01:07:55,225 --> 01:07:57,136
Incredible.

934
01:07:58,106 --> 01:08:00,462
It's even worse
than I thought it would be.

935
01:08:01,627 --> 01:08:05,747
<i>Greetings. This is a recorded
announcement as we are all out.</i>

936
01:08:05,829 --> 01:08:09,140
<i>The Commercial Council of Magrathea
thanks you for your visit,</i>

937
01:08:09,270 --> 01:08:12,468
<i>but regrets that the entire planet
is temporarily closed.</i>

938
01:08:12,551 --> 01:08:16,182
<i>If you'd like to leave your name
and a planet where you can be contacted,</i>

939
01:08:16,273 --> 01:08:18,310
<i>kindly do so at the tone.</i>

940
01:08:19,354 --> 01:08:23,110
- How can a planet be closed?
- Zarkin' A, cousin.

941
01:08:23,196 --> 01:08:26,473
OK, computer, keep going.
Take us down.

942
01:08:26,597 --> 01:08:28,110
<i>I'd be happy to.</i>

943
01:08:29,678 --> 01:08:34,549
<i>It is gratifying that your enthusiasm
for our planet continues unabated.</i>

944
01:08:34,640 --> 01:08:37,155
<i>As a token of our appreciation,</i>

945
01:08:37,281 --> 01:08:40,194
<i>we hope you will enjoy
the two thermo-nuclear missiles</i>

946
01:08:40,282 --> 01:08:43,400
<i>we've just sent
to converge with your craft.</i>

947
01:08:44,084 --> 01:08:46,394
<i>To ensure on-going quality of service,</i>

948
01:08:46,525 --> 01:08:50,599
<i>your death may be monitored
for training purposes. Thank you.</i>

949
01:08:51,206 --> 01:08:55,326
<i>I'm delighted to tell you there are two
nuclear missiles heading right for us.</i>

950
01:08:55,408 --> 01:08:58,640
<i>If you don't mind,
I'm going to take evasive action.</i>

951
01:09:02,811 --> 01:09:05,884
<i>Something's jamming
my guidance system.</i>

952
01:09:05,972 --> 01:09:07,850
<i>Impact minus 45 seconds.</i>

953
01:09:11,454 --> 01:09:13,571
Computer, do something.

954
01:09:13,695 --> 01:09:17,815
<i>Sure. Handing over manual control.
Good luck.</i>

955
01:09:28,061 --> 01:09:30,017
I need some help.

956
01:09:30,782 --> 01:09:33,217
I can't do this without my third arm.

957
01:09:35,944 --> 01:09:38,095
Arthur, grab my hand.

958
01:09:48,108 --> 01:09:51,659
- Did we lose 'em?
- No, they're coming right at us.

959
01:09:54,231 --> 01:09:57,781
- Why don't I just press this?
- No, we're not back to normal yet.

960
01:09:57,872 --> 01:09:59,829
- What will happen?
- I don't know.

961
01:09:59,913 --> 01:10:03,749
Don't do it. We're here.
No telling where it'll send us.

962
01:10:06,596 --> 01:10:08,473
- No, sod it all.
- No.

963
01:10:14,399 --> 01:10:16,310
Where are we?

964
01:10:20,241 --> 01:10:23,791
- Exactly where we were.
- And the missiles?

965
01:10:24,803 --> 01:10:30,118
Apparently, they've turned
into a bowl of petunias.

966
01:10:31,565 --> 01:10:34,319
And a very surprised-looking whale.

967
01:10:34,446 --> 01:10:41,400
<i>And an improbability factor
of 8,767,128 to 1 against.</i>

968
01:10:42,289 --> 01:10:44,201
We have normality.

969
01:10:45,131 --> 01:10:47,486
Normality, right.

970
01:10:47,571 --> 01:10:50,371
We can talk about normality
till the cows come home.

971
01:10:51,493 --> 01:10:54,406
- What is normal?
- What's home?

972
01:10:54,494 --> 01:10:56,133
What are cows?

973
01:10:57,255 --> 01:11:00,009
A proper cup of tea
would restore my normality.

974
01:11:00,096 --> 01:11:04,171
<i>It is important to note that suddenly,
and against all probability,</i>

975
01:11:04,258 --> 01:11:07,012
<i>a sperm whale had been
called into existence</i>

976
01:11:07,099 --> 01:11:11,060
<i>several miles above
the surface of an alien planet.</i>

977
01:11:11,141 --> 01:11:13,861
<i>Since this is not a tenable position
for a whale,</i>

978
01:11:13,942 --> 01:11:18,734
<i>this innocent creature had very little
time to come to terms with its identity.</i>

979
01:11:18,824 --> 01:11:21,293
<i>This is what it thought as it fell:</i>

980
01:11:21,385 --> 01:11:24,458
<i>What's happening? Who am I?</i>

981
01:11:24,546 --> 01:11:28,062
<i>Why am I here? What's my purpose?
What do I mean by "who am l"?</i>

982
01:11:28,187 --> 01:11:30,224
<i>OK, calm down, get a grip now.</i>

983
01:11:30,308 --> 01:11:34,223
<i>This is an interesting sensation.
A sort of tingling in my...</i>

984
01:11:34,310 --> 01:11:39,146
<i>I better start finding names for things.
Let's call it a tail. Yeah, tail.</i>

985
01:11:39,272 --> 01:11:43,152
<i>What's this roaring sound whooshing past
what I'm gonna call my head?</i>

986
01:11:43,233 --> 01:11:45,270
<i>Wind. Is that a good name? That'll do.</i>

987
01:11:45,354 --> 01:11:48,506
<i>Yay, this is really exciting.
I'm dizzy with anticipation.</i>

988
01:11:48,595 --> 01:11:51,030
<i>Or is it the wind?
There's a lot of that now.</i>

989
01:11:51,116 --> 01:11:55,031
<i>And what's this thing coming towards me
very fast? So big and flat and round.</i>

990
01:11:55,158 --> 01:12:00,029
<i>It needs a big wide-sounding name
like ow, ound, round, ground.</i>

991
01:12:00,120 --> 01:12:04,478
<i>That's it, ground. I wonder if it will
be friends with me. Hello, ground.</i>

992
01:12:06,762 --> 01:12:10,393
<i>The only thing that went through
the mind of the bowl of petunias</i>

993
01:12:10,484 --> 01:12:14,364
<i>as it fell was, "Oh, no, not again."</i>

994
01:12:15,566 --> 01:12:20,961
<i>Many have speculated that if we knew
why the petunias had thought that,</i>

995
01:12:21,048 --> 01:12:26,249
<i>we should know a lot more about the
nature of the universe than we do now.</i>

996
01:12:30,371 --> 01:12:32,522
What are those things?

997
01:12:32,612 --> 01:12:35,844
They're portals to another dimension.

998
01:12:35,933 --> 01:12:40,247
Right. I don't suppose this portal
has central heating, does it?

999
01:12:46,057 --> 01:12:47,776
This is the one.

1000
01:12:47,858 --> 01:12:52,138
This will all end in tears,
I just know it.

1001
01:12:54,901 --> 01:12:56,972
Right here.

1002
01:13:01,183 --> 01:13:03,573
OK, in we go.

1003
01:13:03,664 --> 01:13:08,581
We can't just step into that... that.
We don't know where it leads.

1004
01:13:08,666 --> 01:13:10,703
If we pick the wrong one,

1005
01:13:10,827 --> 01:13:14,377
we just come back,
we pick another one. It's no biggie.

1006
01:13:14,508 --> 01:13:17,946
What? Yeah, it's a big biggie, Ford.
A big biggie.

1007
01:13:18,030 --> 01:13:22,822
What if it rips us all into
tiny little atomic particle thingies?

1008
01:13:22,912 --> 01:13:26,303
This is the right one. I have a hunch.

1009
01:13:26,393 --> 01:13:30,434
- Ford.
- His hunches are good.

1010
01:13:30,515 --> 01:13:32,825
Arthur, I say we go.

1011
01:13:32,916 --> 01:13:38,550
Go with the hunch of a man
whose brain is fuelled by lemons?

1012
01:13:38,638 --> 01:13:40,230
This is suicide.

1013
01:13:40,318 --> 01:13:44,154
- I think I resent that.
- I don't care.

1014
01:13:44,240 --> 01:13:47,039
I'm getting hostility from you, Alex...

1015
01:13:47,121 --> 01:13:50,558
- Arthur.
- Have you ever tried yoga?

1016
01:13:51,683 --> 01:13:55,154
- Trillian.
- I think that's supposed to happen.

1017
01:13:59,806 --> 01:14:02,002
She's gone. Ford, she's gone.

1018
01:14:03,127 --> 01:14:05,323
Arthur, don't panic.

1019
01:14:07,649 --> 01:14:11,438
- Ford.
- Hey, wait for me.

1020
01:14:24,456 --> 01:14:26,128
Come on.

1021
01:14:26,216 --> 01:14:28,128
Idiot. Come on.

1022
01:14:52,186 --> 01:14:55,703
No, don't leave.
Come on, just start.

1023
01:14:55,788 --> 01:14:58,781
I told you this would end in tears.

1024
01:14:58,909 --> 01:15:00,901
Did you?

1025
01:15:09,873 --> 01:15:11,466
Geronimo.

1026
01:15:14,075 --> 01:15:15,667
Far out.

1027
01:15:18,436 --> 01:15:20,507
This is it.

1028
01:15:20,597 --> 01:15:22,953
Deep Thought. I'm a-coming.

1029
01:15:25,639 --> 01:15:30,271
So, this is how it's all going to end,
is it?

1030
01:15:32,562 --> 01:15:36,522
Me, alone on a dead planet,
with a manically depressed robot.

1031
01:15:36,603 --> 01:15:38,481
You think you've got problems.

1032
01:15:38,564 --> 01:15:41,477
What if you are
a manically depressed robot?

1033
01:15:41,605 --> 01:15:43,517
Excuse me.

1034
01:15:49,528 --> 01:15:53,967
- Who are you?
- No, my name is not important.

1035
01:15:55,131 --> 01:15:57,487
You must come with me.

1036
01:15:57,572 --> 01:16:01,691
- Get away.
- Terrible events are afoot.

1037
01:16:01,773 --> 01:16:05,131
You must come or you'll be late.

1038
01:16:05,215 --> 01:16:07,410
- Late? What for?
- What?

1039
01:16:07,495 --> 01:16:12,776
No. What's your name, Earthman?

1040
01:16:13,698 --> 01:16:15,974
- Dent. Arthur Dent.
- Well...

1041
01:16:16,059 --> 01:16:18,699
Well, late as in
"the late Dentarthurdent."

1042
01:16:19,420 --> 01:16:23,131
It's a sort of threat, do you see? No.

1043
01:16:23,262 --> 01:16:26,141
- No.
- Your friends are safe.

1044
01:16:26,223 --> 01:16:29,296
- You can trust me.
- A man who won't tell me his name?

1045
01:16:31,025 --> 01:16:33,938
OK, my name is...

1046
01:16:34,026 --> 01:16:37,781
My name is... is... is...
Slartibartfast.

1047
01:16:39,868 --> 01:16:42,827
I said it wasn't important.

1048
01:16:44,430 --> 01:16:48,470
Well... So my friends are safe?

1049
01:16:48,591 --> 01:16:49,945
Let me show you.

1050
01:16:50,032 --> 01:16:55,711
I could calculate your chances
of survival, but you won't like it.

1051
01:17:19,643 --> 01:17:22,317
Did you know we built planets?

1052
01:17:22,404 --> 01:17:25,443
Oh, yes, fascinating trade.

1053
01:17:26,366 --> 01:17:30,839
Doing the coastlines
was always my favourite.

1054
01:17:30,928 --> 01:17:35,879
We used to have endless fun doing the
little fiddly bits around the fjords.

1055
01:17:35,970 --> 01:17:41,251
But then the galactic economy collapsed,
and seeing that custom-built planets

1056
01:17:41,332 --> 01:17:45,212
are a bit of a luxury commodity...

1057
01:17:46,694 --> 01:17:50,404
Come with me because there's
been a terrible mix-up with your planet.

1058
01:17:50,495 --> 01:17:52,646
Best laid plans of mice, you know.

1059
01:17:52,736 --> 01:17:56,048
- And men.
- What?

1060
01:17:56,138 --> 01:17:58,094
Best laid plans of mice and men.

1061
01:17:59,779 --> 01:18:02,738
Yes, well, I don't think men
have much to do with it. Here.

1062
01:18:41,915 --> 01:18:43,952
I must warn you,

1063
01:18:44,036 --> 01:18:47,347
we're going to pass through,
well, a sort of gateway thing.

1064
01:18:47,477 --> 01:18:49,594
It may disturb you.

1065
01:18:49,678 --> 01:18:51,749
It scares the willies out of me.

1066
01:19:22,011 --> 01:19:25,448
Welcome to our factory floor.

1067
01:19:48,261 --> 01:19:50,173
All right.

1068
01:19:51,662 --> 01:19:54,416
Yeah, this is it.

1069
01:19:57,345 --> 01:19:59,734
This is it.

1070
01:19:59,865 --> 01:20:03,576
O great Deep Thought.

1071
01:20:03,667 --> 01:20:05,306
We have travelled long...

1072
01:20:07,308 --> 01:20:08,981
...and far.

1073
01:20:09,109 --> 01:20:14,344
Have you calculated
the ultimate question of life,

1074
01:20:14,431 --> 01:20:16,741
the universe and everything?

1075
01:20:17,552 --> 01:20:18,542
<i>No.</i>

1076
01:20:18,633 --> 01:20:21,068
<i>- What?
- I've been watching TV.</i>

1077
01:20:21,914 --> 01:20:24,383
<i>I designed
another computer to do that.</i>

1078
01:20:25,876 --> 01:20:28,106
Oh, right, I forgot. Is it here?

1079
01:20:28,196 --> 01:20:30,836
<i>No, it's not here. It's another world.</i>

1080
01:20:30,918 --> 01:20:32,954
It's on another world.

1081
01:20:33,078 --> 01:20:36,436
<i>It is another world, stupid.</i>

1082
01:20:36,520 --> 01:20:38,830
<i>Or it was until the Vogons destroyed it</i>

1083
01:20:38,921 --> 01:20:41,197
<i>to make way
for a hyperspace expressway.</i>

1084
01:20:41,402 --> 01:20:43,518
Well... OK.

1085
01:20:43,602 --> 01:20:45,639
You sure you don't have the question,

1086
01:20:45,723 --> 01:20:49,081
or a way to, you know,
access it or something?

1087
01:20:49,165 --> 01:20:51,042
I think I've done a lot to get here.

1088
01:20:51,165 --> 01:20:54,398
<i>Oh, shh. The show's back on.</i>

1089
01:20:59,609 --> 01:21:03,523
Well, I don't wanna bother you,
so I'm gonna... Good stuff.

1090
01:21:03,610 --> 01:21:07,844
Great. I'm gonna go find something
else for my entire life to be about.

1091
01:21:07,932 --> 01:21:11,084
Zaphod, there's the gun.

1092
01:21:11,173 --> 01:21:13,369
- Gun?
- That Humma sent you for.

1093
01:21:13,454 --> 01:21:17,768
- You gave him your head.
- Why'd I do that?

1094
01:21:17,856 --> 01:21:19,448
- OK.
- That's stupid.

1095
01:21:19,576 --> 01:21:24,447
O Deep Thought,
we were told that there is a gun.

1096
01:21:38,664 --> 01:21:40,815
There you are.

1097
01:21:40,905 --> 01:21:42,497
Look familiar?

1098
01:21:42,585 --> 01:21:45,977
So it wasn't destroyed?

1099
01:21:46,067 --> 01:21:50,141
Actually, it was.
This is a backup. Earth Mark II.

1100
01:21:53,149 --> 01:21:54,868
So you made the Earth?

1101
01:21:55,790 --> 01:21:58,430
Not me alone, but I did my part.

1102
01:21:58,551 --> 01:22:01,704
Ever heard of a place,
I think it's called Norway?

1103
01:22:01,793 --> 01:22:05,503
That was one of mine.
I... I got an award for it.

1104
01:22:14,798 --> 01:22:18,109
All right, Frank? That's Frank.

1105
01:22:20,040 --> 01:22:22,396
They've nearly finished the oceans.

1106
01:22:31,444 --> 01:22:32,719
Zaphod.

1107
01:22:32,805 --> 01:22:34,716
Here, it's...

1108
01:22:37,126 --> 01:22:40,040
It's just like Humma said.

1109
01:22:40,128 --> 01:22:42,597
- Now you can go get your head back.
- Hey, Ford.

1110
01:22:42,689 --> 01:22:45,079
It's been nice knowing you,
you zarkin' frood.

1111
01:22:45,170 --> 01:22:46,159
No.

1112
01:22:47,210 --> 01:22:49,361
This is a bust too.

1113
01:22:49,451 --> 01:22:53,492
Hey, man. You know,
you must really be frustrated.

1114
01:22:53,573 --> 01:22:56,486
You go through all you have,
you don't get an answer,

1115
01:22:56,574 --> 01:22:59,214
which means no money or fame,
which you deserve...

1116
01:22:59,295 --> 01:23:04,291
<i>The Point-of-View Gun, conveniently,
does precisely what its name suggests.</i>

1117
01:23:04,417 --> 01:23:06,807
Fantastic.

1118
01:23:07,898 --> 01:23:10,732
<i>If you point it at someone
and pull the trigger,</i>

1119
01:23:10,859 --> 01:23:13,500
<i>they instantly see things
from your point of view.</i>

1120
01:23:13,581 --> 01:23:15,333
Give me that thing.

1121
01:23:15,461 --> 01:23:17,293
<i>It was designed by Deep Thought,</i>

1122
01:23:17,382 --> 01:23:21,137
<i>but commissioned by a consortium
of intergalactic angry housewives</i>

1123
01:23:21,224 --> 01:23:23,533
<i>who, after arguments
with their husbands,</i>

1124
01:23:23,624 --> 01:23:26,857
<i>were sick of ending
those arguments with the phrase,</i>

1125
01:23:26,986 --> 01:23:28,978
<i>"You just don't get it, do you?"</i>

1126
01:23:31,668 --> 01:23:33,818
And you're right,
I shouldn't be so upset

1127
01:23:33,908 --> 01:23:38,267
because life goes on, you know,
and I should just go on with it.

1128
01:23:38,870 --> 01:23:41,101
Shoot him again.

1129
01:23:44,232 --> 01:23:47,863
Hitchhiking's good. Towels are good too.

1130
01:23:47,954 --> 01:23:51,106
- Say what?
- That is brilliant.

1131
01:23:51,195 --> 01:23:53,187
What's goin'...?

1132
01:23:53,276 --> 01:23:55,950
I can see why Humma Kavula
would want one of these.

1133
01:23:57,157 --> 01:23:59,752
- We better get back. Arthur's waiting.
- Who cares?

1134
01:23:59,838 --> 01:24:04,197
I do. Especially since we're somewhat
of an endangered species, thanks to you.

1135
01:24:04,280 --> 01:24:06,317
Why so edgy, baby doll? Relax.

1136
01:24:06,441 --> 01:24:09,115
You wanna know why I'm edgy?

1137
01:24:10,403 --> 01:24:12,998
Of course you're edgy.
Your planet's been blown up

1138
01:24:13,084 --> 01:24:16,794
and you've been tooling around
with the guy who signed the order.

1139
01:24:18,886 --> 01:24:20,957
You actually wanted
to know the question

1140
01:24:21,047 --> 01:24:23,687
because you always wondered
if there was more to life

1141
01:24:23,768 --> 01:24:27,523
and now you're crushed
because you find out there really isn't.

1142
01:24:27,609 --> 01:24:31,968
Hey, fantastic. Psychedelic.

1143
01:24:33,692 --> 01:24:37,322
You've got no home, no family,
and you're stuck with me,

1144
01:24:37,413 --> 01:24:40,884
another in a long line of men
who doesn't really get you.

1145
01:24:43,215 --> 01:24:45,127
That's not true.

1146
01:24:47,737 --> 01:24:51,937
And you're worried you might have blown
it with the one guy who really does.

1147
01:24:57,261 --> 01:24:59,252
Oh, baby doll.

1148
01:24:59,942 --> 01:25:01,854
Give me that thing.

1149
01:25:05,504 --> 01:25:08,622
It won't affect me.
I'm already a woman.

1150
01:25:19,309 --> 01:25:21,505
It's OK.
It's only a couple of little mice.

1151
01:25:25,112 --> 01:25:28,469
Voilà. Himalayas. Good, eh?

1152
01:25:31,794 --> 01:25:34,025
Earthman, you must realise

1153
01:25:34,155 --> 01:25:37,148
that the planet you lived
on was commissioned,

1154
01:25:37,236 --> 01:25:40,434
paid for and run by mice.

1155
01:25:43,118 --> 01:25:46,237
When you say mice,
do you mean the little furry creatures

1156
01:25:46,320 --> 01:25:48,151
with whiskers, ears, cheese?

1157
01:25:48,240 --> 01:25:53,077
Yeah, but they're protrusions into our
dimension of hyperintelligent beings.

1158
01:25:53,162 --> 01:25:56,520
I don't know this cheese
of which you speak,

1159
01:25:56,644 --> 01:26:00,194
but they were there on Earth
as mice experimenting on you.

1160
01:26:00,285 --> 01:26:04,997
I see where you've become confused now.
You see, we were experimenting on them.

1161
01:26:05,127 --> 01:26:08,803
Ah, no, well, yeah, no.
That's what they wanted you to think,

1162
01:26:08,888 --> 01:26:12,678
but you were actually
elements in their computer program.

1163
01:26:14,331 --> 01:26:16,721
Actually, this explains a lot.

1164
01:26:16,812 --> 01:26:19,201
All my life,
I've had this strange feeling

1165
01:26:19,293 --> 01:26:22,286
there's something big and sinister
going on in the world.

1166
01:26:22,374 --> 01:26:26,368
No, that's normal paranoia.
Everyone in the universe gets that.

1167
01:26:29,216 --> 01:26:32,574
Perhaps I'm old and tired,
but the chances of finding out

1168
01:26:32,658 --> 01:26:35,253
what's actually going on
are so absurdly remote

1169
01:26:35,339 --> 01:26:39,299
that the only thing to do is say hang
the sense of it and keep yourself busy.

1170
01:26:39,380 --> 01:26:41,690
I'd much rather be happy
than right any day.

1171
01:26:41,821 --> 01:26:43,699
- And are you?
- No.

1172
01:26:44,422 --> 01:26:46,334
That's where it all falls down.

1173
01:26:57,187 --> 01:26:59,224
Here you are then.

1174
01:27:13,113 --> 01:27:14,672
Is this...?

1175
01:27:28,119 --> 01:27:30,270
It's all there.

1176
01:27:31,360 --> 01:27:33,511
You know, it all works.

1177
01:27:33,601 --> 01:27:35,593
Welcome home.

1178
01:27:56,650 --> 01:27:58,642
- There you are.
- Surprise.

1179
01:27:58,731 --> 01:28:01,530
What are you doing?
What happened to you?

1180
01:28:01,692 --> 01:28:03,046
Is that tea?

1181
01:28:03,133 --> 01:28:07,286
- First, our hosts attacked us.
- Right.

1182
01:28:07,374 --> 01:28:10,686
But then they made up for it
by making us

1183
01:28:10,776 --> 01:28:13,007
this amazing meal.

1184
01:28:13,857 --> 01:28:17,055
It's so... Everything's right.
Everything's delicious.

1185
01:28:18,619 --> 01:28:21,180
Oh, come on, that's lovely.

1186
01:28:21,260 --> 01:28:23,456
We're glad you like it, Earth creature.

1187
01:28:27,462 --> 01:28:31,980
The talking mice. Cool.

1188
01:28:32,064 --> 01:28:36,981
- Sit, Earthman.
- Oh, thank you. Thank you for that.

1189
01:28:37,906 --> 01:28:41,218
- Please, drink.
- Excuse me.

1190
01:28:41,308 --> 01:28:44,540
- Is there anything else?
- No, we're quite happy, thank you.

1191
01:28:44,629 --> 01:28:48,179
Good, I'll be outside, so...

1192
01:28:48,270 --> 01:28:50,421
You were right.

1193
01:28:50,511 --> 01:28:52,230
- Please, drink.
- Yes.

1194
01:28:52,832 --> 01:28:54,710
He was right.

1195
01:28:54,833 --> 01:28:56,585
Now, to business.

1196
01:28:56,673 --> 01:28:58,392
To business.

1197
01:28:58,474 --> 01:28:59,749
Eat.

1198
01:29:00,955 --> 01:29:02,150
Sorry.

1199
01:29:02,236 --> 01:29:05,991
We've spent a lot of time on your planet
looking for this ultimate question.

1200
01:29:06,077 --> 01:29:09,514
Only to have it blow up in our faces.
Literally.

1201
01:29:09,598 --> 01:29:12,956
Which is why you're here.
We've been offered a lucrative contract

1202
01:29:13,040 --> 01:29:14,997
to do several 5-D TV chat shows.

1203
01:29:15,081 --> 01:29:17,516
But here's the point.
We must have product.

1204
01:29:17,602 --> 01:29:20,675
We need the ultimate question,
or one that sounds ultimate.

1205
01:29:20,803 --> 01:29:22,999
- Of course.
- We've rebuilt the planet.

1206
01:29:23,084 --> 01:29:25,838
Now all we need
is the missing piece of the puzzle.

1207
01:29:25,925 --> 01:29:28,042
- Which happens to be your brain.
- Right.

1208
01:29:28,126 --> 01:29:29,320
More tea?

1209
01:29:29,446 --> 01:29:32,041
Sorry, did you just say
you need my brain?

1210
01:29:32,127 --> 01:29:34,323
Yes, to complete the programme.

1211
01:29:37,169 --> 01:29:39,764
You can't have my brain. I'm using it.

1212
01:29:39,890 --> 01:29:44,522
- Hardly.
- Hardly? Cheeky mouse.

1213
01:29:48,854 --> 01:29:51,289
Zaphod. Trillian.

1214
01:29:51,375 --> 01:29:53,571
Ford.

1215
01:29:53,656 --> 01:29:55,089
What was in that food?

1216
01:29:56,657 --> 01:29:58,774
What was in my tea?

1217
01:29:58,858 --> 01:30:01,327
Don't worry. You won't feel a thing.

1218
01:30:04,660 --> 01:30:06,458
Just wait a sodding minute.

1219
01:30:06,541 --> 01:30:09,136
You want a question
that goes with the answer 42?

1220
01:30:09,222 --> 01:30:10,814
What about, what's 6x7?

1221
01:30:10,902 --> 01:30:14,294
Or how many Vogons
does it take to change a light bulb?

1222
01:30:14,384 --> 01:30:18,014
Here's one: How many roads
must a man walk down?

1223
01:30:19,346 --> 01:30:21,257
Hey, that's not bad.

1224
01:30:21,346 --> 01:30:27,458
Fine, take it, cos my head
is filled with questions,

1225
01:30:27,589 --> 01:30:29,740
and no answer to any one of them

1226
01:30:29,830 --> 01:30:33,301
has ever brought me
one iota of happiness.

1227
01:30:33,391 --> 01:30:34,905
Except for one.

1228
01:30:34,992 --> 01:30:37,791
The one. The only question
I've ever wanted an answer to.

1229
01:30:37,873 --> 01:30:39,465
Is she the one?

1230
01:30:40,514 --> 01:30:43,791
The answer bloody well isn't 42,
it's yes.

1231
01:30:43,875 --> 01:30:49,190
Undoubtedly, unequivocally,
unabashedly, yes.

1232
01:30:49,277 --> 01:30:52,031
And for one week,

1233
01:30:52,158 --> 01:30:56,711
one week in my sad little blip
of an existence,

1234
01:30:58,881 --> 01:31:00,873
it made me happy.

1235
01:31:02,962 --> 01:31:04,316
That's a good answer.

1236
01:31:04,403 --> 01:31:07,476
Rubbish. We don't want to be happy.
We want to be famous.

1237
01:31:07,564 --> 01:31:11,081
- What's this "Is she the one" tripe?
- Take his brain.

1238
01:31:11,166 --> 01:31:14,239
No, don't take the brain.
Don't take the brain, mice.

1239
01:31:23,410 --> 01:31:26,404
It wasn't me. I'm famous.

1240
01:31:26,492 --> 01:31:28,767
Shoot him, quickly, shoot him.

1241
01:31:28,852 --> 01:31:30,252
Oh, bollocks.

1242
01:31:38,936 --> 01:31:40,416
I'm gonna be sick.

1243
01:31:43,858 --> 01:31:45,178
Belgium.

1244
01:31:53,302 --> 01:31:54,735
Follow me.

1245
01:31:55,303 --> 01:31:57,693
Left, right, left, right.

1246
01:31:57,784 --> 01:32:00,219
Attention.

1247
01:32:03,546 --> 01:32:06,380
Thanks a lot
for leaving me behind.

1248
01:32:06,507 --> 01:32:08,624
Though I can't say I blame you.

1249
01:32:08,708 --> 01:32:12,258
Mr President,
we're here for your protection.

1250
01:32:12,349 --> 01:32:14,580
Thanks, man. I appreciate it.

1251
01:32:14,670 --> 01:32:15,945
Fire.

1252
01:32:17,711 --> 01:32:19,225
No.

1253
01:32:22,153 --> 01:32:24,190
What's all the fuss about?

1254
01:32:24,274 --> 01:32:26,994
Vogons are the worst marksmen
in the galaxy.

1255
01:32:27,075 --> 01:32:31,992
- For God's sake, stop.
- Cease fire.

1256
01:32:38,359 --> 01:32:40,271
Now I've got a headache.

1257
01:32:50,564 --> 01:32:52,442
Marvin.

1258
01:32:52,565 --> 01:32:53,759
Oh, no.

1259
01:32:55,926 --> 01:32:59,044
He's got a towel, run away.

1260
01:33:01,168 --> 01:33:04,047
I'm gonna get us outta here.
How do you drive this thing?

1261
01:33:04,129 --> 01:33:05,404
What's going on?

1262
01:33:07,731 --> 01:33:09,608
We need that gun.

1263
01:33:10,732 --> 01:33:13,531
Come on. Get this ship started.
Come on. Start.

1264
01:33:14,613 --> 01:33:16,570
Come on. Oh, fire, fire.

1265
01:33:16,654 --> 01:33:17,929
Fire, fire.

1266
01:33:22,376 --> 01:33:24,493
He's locked it from the other side.

1267
01:33:24,577 --> 01:33:26,648
We'll have to go the other way.

1268
01:33:26,738 --> 01:33:28,570
Come on, let's go.

1269
01:33:33,701 --> 01:33:36,057
Stop.

1270
01:33:41,864 --> 01:33:45,859
Stop it. Stop shooting at us.

1271
01:34:04,153 --> 01:34:07,703
Oh, I feel so depressed.

1272
01:34:09,035 --> 01:34:13,746
I can't face another day.

1273
01:34:15,557 --> 01:34:17,276
What's the point?

1274
01:34:25,001 --> 01:34:27,197
- You all right?
- Yeah.

1275
01:34:32,644 --> 01:34:35,398
Sorry about your spaceship, Arthur.

1276
01:34:40,727 --> 01:34:42,844
Marvin, you saved our lives.

1277
01:34:42,928 --> 01:34:46,080
I know. Wretched, isn't it?

1278
01:34:51,331 --> 01:34:54,244
You got to ask yourself,
what's the point?

1279
01:34:54,372 --> 01:34:56,329
Take it away.

1280
01:35:04,256 --> 01:35:09,332
You could do all of that, but it's
not gonna... I'll ask... I'll just ask.

1281
01:35:09,418 --> 01:35:13,048
I've just been informed
by some of the lads

1282
01:35:13,179 --> 01:35:15,455
that since we're so near completion,

1283
01:35:15,540 --> 01:35:17,850
we're gonna go ahead and finish Earth.

1284
01:35:19,502 --> 01:35:23,337
Well, we can put it back exactly
as it was when you left, you know.

1285
01:35:23,423 --> 01:35:26,656
Unless there's...

1286
01:35:26,745 --> 01:35:30,136
there's, you know,
anything you want to change.

1287
01:35:32,147 --> 01:35:37,269
Something you think
your planet could do without.

1288
01:35:38,669 --> 01:35:41,787
Yeah. Me.

1289
01:35:42,271 --> 01:35:44,387
Oh. Hello?

1290
01:35:44,472 --> 01:35:48,068
No, as is. Yep, leave it. Yep.

1291
01:35:49,954 --> 01:35:51,467
Let's go somewhere.

1292
01:35:51,554 --> 01:35:54,023
Definitely. Where did you have in mind?

1293
01:35:54,115 --> 01:35:57,233
I know this great restaurant
at the end of the universe.

1294
01:35:57,997 --> 01:36:00,228
I am a little peckish.

1295
01:36:02,158 --> 01:36:04,832
I need to go to Humma's.
He's got something of mine.

1296
01:36:04,919 --> 01:36:10,315
- I think. I'm so confused.
- Don't worry, baby. We'll sort it out.

1297
01:36:10,442 --> 01:36:13,913
All right, let's trip the light
fantastic, baby. Just you and me.

1298
01:36:14,003 --> 01:36:15,756
Come on.

1299
01:36:15,884 --> 01:36:19,764
- You got your towel?
- Yeah. Why? Am I going to need it?

1300
01:36:19,885 --> 01:36:23,162
- Only always.
- Right. Yeah.

1301
01:36:23,247 --> 01:36:27,446
I wouldn't want to go
anywhere without my wonderful towel.

1302
01:36:31,930 --> 01:36:34,684
OK. Hold tight.

1303
01:36:38,052 --> 01:36:43,048
<i>Stand by for commencement
of life cycle. In three, two, one...</i>

1304
01:36:43,134 --> 01:36:44,887
<i>All right.</i>

1305
01:37:27,351 --> 01:37:29,547
Not that anyone cares what I say,

1306
01:37:29,632 --> 01:37:32,750
but the restaurant
is at the other end of the universe.

1307
01:38:00,524 --> 01:38:03,996
<i>So long and thanks for all the fish</i>

1308
01:38:04,126 --> 01:38:08,040
<i>So sad that it should come to this</i>

1309
01:38:08,167 --> 01:38:12,799
<i>We tried to warn you all but, oh, dear</i>

1310
01:38:15,690 --> 01:38:19,571
<i>You may not share our intellect</i>

1311
01:38:19,652 --> 01:38:23,362
<i>Which might explain your disrespect</i>

1312
01:38:23,493 --> 01:38:30,572
<i>For all the natural wonders
that grow around you</i>

1313
01:38:30,656 --> 01:38:35,413
<i>So long, so long and thanks
for all the fish</i>

1314
01:38:42,701 --> 01:38:46,456
<i>The world's about to be destroyed</i>

1315
01:38:46,542 --> 01:38:50,218
<i>There's no point getting all annoyed</i>

1316
01:38:50,304 --> 01:38:56,336
<i>Lie back and let the planet dissolve</i>

1317
01:38:57,986 --> 01:39:01,776
<i>Despite those nets of tuna fleets</i>

1318
01:39:01,908 --> 01:39:05,584
<i>We thought that most of you were sweet</i>

1319
01:39:05,709 --> 01:39:12,709
<i>Especially tiny tots
and your pregnant women</i>

1320
01:39:12,792 --> 01:39:16,582
<i>So long, so long,
so long, so long, so long</i>

1321
01:39:20,555 --> 01:39:25,347
<i>So long, so long and thanks
for all the fish</i>

1322
01:39:30,959 --> 01:39:32,188
Yeah.

1323
01:39:42,884 --> 01:39:44,283
Play it.

1324
01:39:47,726 --> 01:39:51,686
<i>So long and thanks for all the fish</i>

1325
01:39:51,767 --> 01:39:55,523
<i>So sad that it should come to this</i>

1326
01:39:55,609 --> 01:40:01,368
<i>We tried to warn you all but, oh, dear</i>

1327
01:40:01,491 --> 01:40:03,084
<i>Oh, dear</i>

1328
01:40:03,172 --> 01:40:06,961
<i>Despite those nets of tuna fleets</i>

1329
01:40:07,053 --> 01:40:10,889
<i>We thought that most of you were sweet</i>

1330
01:40:11,015 --> 01:40:18,014
<i>Especially tiny tots
and your pregnant women</i>

1331
01:40:18,098 --> 01:40:21,853
<i>So long, so long,
so long, so long, so long</i>

1332
01:40:25,700 --> 01:40:33,417
<i>So long, so long and thanks
for all the fish</i>

1333
01:40:34,264 --> 01:40:36,825
<i>For all the fish</i>

1334
01:40:40,186 --> 01:40:44,261
<i>It is, of course, well known
that careless talk costs lives.</i>

1335
01:40:44,348 --> 01:40:47,546
<i>But the full scale of the problem
is not always appreciated.</i>

1336
01:40:47,629 --> 01:40:50,906
<i>For instance, at the very moment
that Arthur Dent said,</i>

1337
01:40:50,990 --> 01:40:54,382
<i>"I wouldn't want to go anywhere
without my wonderful towel",</i>

1338
01:40:54,472 --> 01:40:58,864
<i>a freak wormhole opened up in
the fabric of the space-time continuum</i>

1339
01:40:58,953 --> 01:41:02,311
<i>and carried his words far,
far back in time,</i>

1340
01:41:02,395 --> 01:41:06,275
<i>across almost infinite reaches of space,
to a distant galaxy</i>

1341
01:41:06,356 --> 01:41:09,873
<i>where strange and warlike beings
were poised on the brink</i>

1342
01:41:09,958 --> 01:41:12,348
<i>of frightful interstellar battle.</i>

1343
01:41:12,439 --> 01:41:16,797
<i>The two opposing leaders, resplendent
in their black-jewelled battle shorts</i>

1344
01:41:16,880 --> 01:41:18,837
<i>were meeting for the last time,</i>

1345
01:41:19,001 --> 01:41:21,641
<i>when a dreadful silence fell.</i>

1346
01:41:21,722 --> 01:41:23,600
<i>And at that very moment,</i>

1347
01:41:23,683 --> 01:41:27,472
<i>the words "I wouldn't want to go
anywhere without my wonderful towel"</i>

1348
01:41:27,644 --> 01:41:30,479
<i>drifted across the conference table.</i>

1349
01:41:30,566 --> 01:41:33,127
<i>Unfortunately, in their native tongue,</i>

1350
01:41:33,207 --> 01:41:36,086
<i>this was the most appalling
insult imaginable.</i>

1351
01:41:36,168 --> 01:41:37,726
<i>So the opposing battle fleets</i>

1352
01:41:37,808 --> 01:41:40,323
<i>decided to settle
their remaining differences,</i>

1353
01:41:40,409 --> 01:41:43,642
<i>in order to launch
a joint attack on our galaxy,</i>

1354
01:41:43,731 --> 01:41:47,691
<i>now positively identified
as the source of the offending remark.</i>

1355
01:41:47,772 --> 01:41:50,890
<i>For thousands of years,
the mighty starships tore across</i>

1356
01:41:51,014 --> 01:41:56,215
<i>the empty wastes of space and finally
dived screaming onto the planet Earth,</i>

1357
01:41:56,296 --> 01:42:00,176
<i>where, due to
a terrible miscalculation of scale,</i>

1358
01:42:00,297 --> 01:42:04,895
<i>the entire battle fleet was
accidentally swallowed by a small dog.</i>

1359
01:42:04,979 --> 01:42:08,416
<i>Those who study the complex
interplay of cause and effect</i>

1360
01:42:08,540 --> 01:42:10,179
<i>in the history of the universe</i>

1361
01:42:10,301 --> 01:42:14,615
<i>say that this sort of thing
is going on all the time.</i>
